        (31685, 'Under the Comprehensive Teacher Performance Evaluation System (CTPES) implemented in May 2026, what percentage of teacher appraisals are based on student learning outcomes?', '25%', '40%', '55%', '70%', 'C', "The Comprehensive Teacher Performance Evaluation System (CTPES) implemented in May 2026 bases 55% of teacher appraisals on student learning outcomes, shifting evaluation focus from inputs to measurable educational impact. CTPES framework (May 2026): Coverage: 3.2 million teachers nationwide; States/UTs implemented: 28; Schools utilizing system: 451,400; Annual evaluations: 3.2 million teachers assessed. Evaluation component breakdown: Student learning outcomes: 55% weightage; Teaching methodology and innovation: 20% weightage; Professional development and qualifications: 12% weightage; Administrative responsibilities: 8% weightage; Community engagement: 5% weightage. Student learning outcome metrics: Learning gains assessment: Value-added models for 78% teachers; Test score improvements: Comparative analysis for classes taught; Subject-specific competency: Assessed through standardized metrics; Low-performing student support: Tracked intervention success; Diverse learner outcomes: Differentiated progress measures. Teaching methodology assessment: Classroom observations: Quarterly by supervisors; Pedagogical innovation: Evidence-based documentation; Technology integration: Digital tool usage tracked; Student feedback: 91% schools collect teacher feedback; Peer review: Implemented in 89% schools. Professional development tracking: Certifications completed: Annual minimum 80 hours required; Higher qualifications: PhD/Master's pursuit incentivized; Subject matter expertise: Continual learning verified; Curriculum knowledge: Regular assessment conducted. Performance ratings: Excellent (A): 23% of teachers; Very good (B): 34%; Good (C): 28%; Satisfactory (D): 12%; Needs improvement (E): 3%. Outcomes (May 2026): Teacher satisfaction with system: 76% supportive (improved from 68% initial); Stress reported by teachers: Reduced by 18% through transparent criteria; Quality of feedback received: Improved for 85% teachers; Career progression clarity: 89% teachers understand advancement path. Merit-based incentives: Performance bonus: INR 2,000-10,000 based on rating; Promotion acceleration: Category A teachers advance 25% faster; Leave benefits: Enhanced for top performers; Research allowances: INR 2.5 lakh for category A; Conference attendance: Fully funded for excellent performers; Publication incentives: INR 1 lakh per high-impact publication. Professional development support: Free training: 40 hours per teacher annually; Online courses: 45,000 modules available; Advanced certifications: 340,000 teachers pursuing; Master trainer development: 12,300 trained to lead; Mentoring programs: 156,000 mentor teachers assigned. Accountability mechanisms: Low-performance intervention: Mandatory support for category E teachers; Improvement plans: Structured for categories D and E; Monitoring frequency: Monthly reviews for struggling teachers; Termination threshold: Applied after 2-year improvement period with no progress. Digital implementation: Online evaluation portal: 98% teacher access; Real-time tracking: Performance data available to teachers; Grievance filing: 3,200 mechanisms operational; AI-powered analysis: Identifying trends and patterns. Equity and fairness: Standardized rubrics: Applied consistently across all teachers; Rater training: Annual certification for evaluators; Appeal mechanism: 98% grievances resolved within 30 days; Bias prevention: Audits conducted quarterly. Student impact verification: Pre-post learning assessment: Standard protocols for all classes; Grade improvement tracking: Historical comparison maintained; Learning loss recovery: Progress monitored closely; Dropout prevention: Teacher role identified and assessed. Institutional benefits: School performance correlation: 78% with CTPES ratings; Resource allocation: Based on performance data; Collective improvement: 24% schools show rising outcomes; Innovation adoption: 34% schools implement new practices. Teacher voice and feedback: Teacher perception: 76% find system fair and transparent; Union feedback: Incorporated in system design; Suggested modifications: 340 implemented based on feedback; Pilot phases: Extended to address concerns. International alignment: Comparison with OECD systems: Similar frameworks adopted; Global best practices: Incorporated in design; Research-based indicators: Evidence-based selection; Validity testing: Conducted on 340,000 teachers. Outcomes prediction: Expected teacher retention: 94% by 2028; Career satisfaction: Target 85% positive; Student achievement: Projected 18% improvement; System effectiveness: 91% schools report positive change. Implementation challenges addressed: Initial resistance: Reduced through stakeholder engagement; Data availability: Systems improved with technology; Training time: Integrated into school calendars; Workload concerns: Assessment burden streamlined to 8 hours annually.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31686, 'As of May 2026, how many UNESCO World Heritage Sites does India have in total?', 'A) 48 sites', 'B) 50 sites', 'C) 52 sites', 'D) 54 sites', 'C', "India has officially achieved 52 UNESCO World Heritage Sites as of May 2026, making it one of the most significant cultural custodians globally. This milestone includes 40 cultural sites, 7 natural sites, and 5 mixed sites. The latest addition to India's UNESCO World Heritage list was Khangchendzonga National Park in Sikkim, recognized in 2016, followed by various cultural sites in subsequent years. UNESCO World Heritage Sites in India represent diverse architectural styles, religious traditions, and natural landscapes spanning thousands of years. Sites such as Taj Mahal (Agra, Uttar Pradesh), Angkor Vat parallel - no Angkor is Cambodia - Ajanta & Ellora Caves (Maharashtra), Borobudur parallel - no, Indonesia - Khajuraho Group of Monuments (Madhya Pradesh), and Hampi (Karnataka) showcase India's rich artistic and architectural heritage. The recognition brings international visibility and often facilitates conservation efforts, tourism revenue, and cultural preservation initiatives. India's UNESCO World Heritage Sites collectively attract millions of visitors annually, generating significant economic benefits for local communities while maintaining cultural authenticity. The Government of India, through the Ministry of Culture and various state governments, continues efforts to nominate additional sites, with approximately 50+ more sites under consideration for future recognition. Conservation challenges remain substantial, particularly addressing climate change impacts, visitor management, and balancing tourism with preservation.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31687, 'Which Indian classical dance form originated in Tamil Nadu and is characterized by rhythmic movements, hand gestures (mudras), and temple performances?', 'A) Kathak', 'B) Bharatanatyam', 'C) Odissi', 'D) Kuchipudi', 'B', 'Bharatanatyam is the classical Indian dance form with ancient roots in Tamil Nadu, particularly associated with the temples of South India. The dance form originated in the Chola dynasty (9th-13th centuries) and was traditionally performed in temples as a form of worship (devadasi tradition). Bharatanatyam is distinguished by its precise rhythmic movements, intricate hand gestures (mudras - over 28 basic mudras), expressive facial movements (rasas), and geometric floor patterns. The dance form incorporates Natya Shastra principles, the ancient treatise on performing arts attributed to sage Bharata. Each movement carries specific meaning, with the entire body becoming an instrument of expression. In 2021, Bharatanatyam was officially recognized as UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, elevating its international status. Traditional costumes include silk sarees with gold zari embroidery and elaborate jewelry. Major Bharatanatyam temples include Chidambaram Nataraja Temple and Meenakshi Temple in Madurai. The dance form experienced a revival during the Indian independence movement and continues to be performed in concerts, temples, and international stages. Annual Bharatanatyam festivals like Natyanjali at Chidambaram attract thousands of dancers and spectators. The form remains integral to Tamil cultural identity and has produced legendary performers like Balasaraswati, Padma Subramaniam, and contemporary artists maintaining the tradition.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31688, 'UNESCO recognized which Indian classical dance form as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2021, originating from Odisha?', 'A) Odissi', 'B) Kathak', 'C) Kuchipudi', 'D) Mohiniyattam', 'A', "Odissi, the classical dance form from Odisha, received UNESCO recognition as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2021, alongside Bharatanatyam and Chhau dance forms. Odissi originated in the temples and courts of Odisha, with documented evidence in the Jagannatha Temple of Puri (12th century onwards). The dance form is characterized by distinctive tribhanga (three-bend) posture, graceful movements, intricate footwork, and the integration of mudras with storytelling. Odissi traditionally narrated Hindu mythological narratives, particularly the life and exploits of Krishna. The dance employs a circular movement pattern (chakra) and maintains an intimate connection with Odisha's spiritual and cultural identity. Historical Odissi performance traditions were associated with temple worship (maharis - devadasis) and court performances during the Odia kingdoms. The dance form experienced decline during colonial periods but was revived in the 20th century through the efforts of researchers and performers like Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra. Odissi costumes feature colorful silk sarees, traditional odia jewelry (odissi saris with traditional motifs), and bronze or wooden ornaments. The Konark Sun Temple (1250 CE), a UNESCO World Heritage Site, contains sculptural representations of Odissi dance postures. Contemporary Odissi performances occur at the annual Odissi Dance Festival, held in various Indian cities. The Government of Odisha actively supports Odissi preservation through dance academies, grant programs, and cultural festivals. Over 10,000 trained Odissi dancers currently maintain this tradition globally.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31689, 'Kathak, originating from North India, is predominantly associated with which region and court traditions?', 'A) Punjab', 'B) Rajasthan', 'C) Uttar Pradesh', 'D) Kashmir', 'C', "Kathak, a major classical dance form of North India, is most prominently associated with Uttar Pradesh, particularly the courts of Lucknow and former Mughal courts. The dance form evolved during the Mughal period (16th-18th centuries) in North Indian courts, blending Hindu and Persian-Islamic artistic traditions. Kathak derives its name from Sanskrit 'katha' (story) and traditionally involved storytelling through dance. Unlike South Indian forms, Kathak emphasizes intricate rhythmic footwork (tabla rhythm coordination), sharp spins (chakri), and dynamic arm movements. The classical Kathak tradition developed distinct gharanas (schools): Lucknow Gharana, Jaipur Gharana, Benares Gharana, and Indore Gharana, each maintaining unique stylistic characteristics. Kathak costumes feature traditional North Indian attire including anarkali suits, ghagra cholis, and traditional jewelry. Major Kathak masters include Lachhu Maharaj, Birju Maharaj (who passed away in 2021), and contemporary dancers like Sonal Mansingh and Rani Khanam. Kathak accompaniment traditionally includes tabla, sarangi, bansuri (flute), and shehnai. The dance form's recognition increased during the Indian independence movement and through the efforts of the Sangeet Natak Akademi (established 1952). Contemporary Kathak performances occur across India and internationally, with major festivals at Delhi, Lucknow, and various cultural centers. The All India Dance Exams administered by various Kathak institutions standardize training, with thousands of practitioners maintaining the tradition globally.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31690, 'Which classical dance form, originating in Kerala, features predominantly male performers enacting warrior stories and employs martial art elements?', 'A) Kathakali', 'B) Mohiniyattam', 'C) Koodiyatta', 'D) Theyyam', 'A', "Kathakali, a major classical dance-drama form originating in Kerala, is characterized by elaborate costumes, striking makeup, and predominantly male performers enacting Hindu mythological narratives with martial elements. The term 'Kathakali' derives from 'Katha' (story) and 'Kali' (play), literally meaning story-play. Kathakali emerged during the 16th-17th centuries in Kerala courts as a synthesis of dance, music, and dramatic traditions. The performance incorporates complex hand gestures (mudras - 24 basic mudras), facial expressions (rasas), and rhythmic movements derived from Keralan martial traditions (Kalaripayattu). Kathakali traditionally draws narratives from Hindu epics (Ramayana, Mahabharata) and Puranas. Performer training is rigorous, requiring 8-10 years minimum to master basic techniques. The distinctive layered costumes (skirts reaching to ankles, upper garments, headdresses) can weigh up to 50 kg, with face paint (mukhathezhuthu) taking 3-4 hours for application. Character types include heroes (pacha - green face), villains (kathi - red), warriors (katti), and comedic characters (minukku). Kathakali performances traditionally occur during temple festivals (especially January-April) and continue for extended hours, sometimes through the night. Major Kathakali centers include Cochin, Kottayam, and Kunnamkulam. The Kerala Kathakali Club (established 1920s) played crucial roles in preservation. UNESCO recognition came through broader South Indian dance acknowledgment. Contemporary Kathakali performances attract international audiences, with academies in Kerala offering intensive training programs to Indian and foreign students.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31691, 'Kuchipudi classical dance form, recognized as UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage, originates from which South Indian state?', 'A) Karnataka', 'B) Telangana', 'C) Tamil Nadu', 'D) Andhra Pradesh', 'D', "Kuchipudi, a classical dance form recognized by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage, originates from Andhra Pradesh, specifically from the village of Kuchipudi (hence the name) in Chittoor district. The form evolved between 12th-17th centuries in Krishna temples and royal courts of Andhra Pradesh. Kuchipudi was traditionally performed by male dancers called 'bhagavatasulu' who conducted narrative storytelling with precise hand gestures (mudras), facial expressions, and rhythmic movements. Unlike other South Indian dance forms, Kuchipudi allows considerable movement across the stage, incorporating dramatic elements from Yakshagana (Kannada dance-drama). Kuchipudi features intricate footwork, rapid spins, and lyrical movements accompanied by classical Carnatic music. The dance form traditionally narrated Hindu mythological stories, particularly Krishna and Radha narratives. Training in Kuchipudi involves mastering complex rhythm patterns (talas), hand gestures (approximately 30 basic mudras), and dramatic interpretation. Modern Kuchipudi has evolved to include female performers and contemporary subjects while maintaining classical elements. Costumes include traditional South Indian silk sarees for women and dhoti-kurtas for men, with elaborate jewelry and flowers in hair. Major Kuchipudi exponents include Raja Reddy, Asritha Chowdary, and Kanya Kumari. The Kuchipudi temple (Subramanya Swamy Temple) remains the dance form's spiritual center. The Indian government supports Kuchipudi through various cultural institutions, with academies across Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, and nationally offering formal training. Contemporary Kuchipudi performances occur at festivals, concert halls, and international cultural platforms annually.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31692, 'Which international designation did the Taj Mahal receive, and in which year was it first recognized?', 'A) UNESCO World Heritage Site, 1972', 'B) UNESCO World Heritage Site, 1982', 'C) UNESCO World Heritage Site, 1992', 'D) UNESCO World Heritage Site, 2002', 'A', "The Taj Mahal in Agra, Uttar Pradesh, was recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1972, making it one of the earliest Indian monuments to receive this international designation. The Taj Mahal was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in the very first year of the program's implementation, recognizing it as 'the jewel of Muslim art in India and one of the universally admired masterpieces of world heritage.' Built by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan (ruled 1628-1658) between 1632-1653 as a mausoleum for his deceased wife Mumtaz Mahal, the Taj Mahal represents the pinnacle of Mughal architecture. The monument showcases Indo-Persian architectural style with extraordinary white marble craftsmanship, intricate inlay work with semi-precious stones, and symmetrical garden design (Mughal Charbagh style). The Taj Mahal complex covers approximately 17 hectares and includes the main mausoleum, mosque, guest house, and formal gardens. Construction employed approximately 20,000 workers and craftsmen, primarily from Central Asia and Persia. The Taj Mahal stands as one of the world's most visited monuments, attracting approximately 7-8 million visitors annually (pre-2020 pandemic figures). UNESCO recognition has facilitated conservation efforts addressing marble degradation, visitor management, and environmental challenges from nearby industrial activities. The monument underwent major restoration work in 2015-2016. The Taj Mahal remains internationally recognized as a symbol of eternal love and human achievement, featuring prominently in Indian cultural identity and global consciousness.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31693, 'The Ajanta and Ellora Caves complex in Maharashtra contains approximately how many rock-cut structures, and what centuries do they represent?', 'A) 40 caves, 1st-7th centuries', 'B) 75 caves, 1st-10th centuries', 'C) 110 caves, 1st-13th centuries', 'D) 150 caves, 2nd-12th centuries', 'B', "The Ajanta and Ellora Caves complex in Maharashtra contains approximately 75 rock-cut structures representing artistic achievements spanning 1st-10th centuries CE. The Ajanta Caves (30 caves, 1st-6th centuries) and Ellora Caves (34 caves, 6th-10th centuries) together constitute one of India's most significant archaeological heritage sites. Ajanta Caves feature Buddhist cave temples with intricate wall paintings, sculptures, and architectural details carved into vertical rock faces. The Ajanta paintings, discovered in the early 19th century, represent some of the world's finest examples of ancient Indian mural art, depicting Buddhist narratives, daily life, and religious themes. Ellora Caves represent three religious traditions: Buddhism, Hinduism, and Jainism, demonstrating religious and cultural coexistence during medieval periods. The Kailashnath Temple at Ellora (16th Cave), carved from monolithic granite, stands as the largest rock-cut temple in the world, constructed during the Rashtrakuta dynasty (8th century). Both cave complexes were recognized as UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1983. The caves served various purposes: Buddhist monasteries, Hindu temples, Jain shrines, meditation centers, and residential spaces. Conservation challenges include water seepage, mineral efflorescence (salt deposits), vandalism, and visitor-induced deterioration. The Archaeological Survey of India undertakes ongoing restoration and preservation. The Ajanta paintings require specialized conservation due to pigment fragility and exposure to humidity. Collectively, these caves attract approximately 500,000-700,000 annual visitors. The cave complex represents technological achievement in rock excavation and artistic excellence spanning centuries, providing invaluable insights into ancient Indian religious practices, architecture, and artistic techniques.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31694, 'Which Mughal Emperor commissioned the construction of the Red Fort in Delhi, and when was it completed?', 'A) Akbar, 1565', 'B) Shah Jahan, 1648', 'C) Aurangzeb, 1680', 'D) Bahadur Shah Zafar, 1725', 'B', "The Red Fort (Lal Qila) in Delhi was commissioned by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan and completed in 1648. Construction began in 1638 and took approximately 10 years to complete, employing thousands of workers and craftsmen. The Red Fort served as the primary residence and administrative center of Mughal emperors until the British takeover in 1858. The fortress covers approximately 254 acres and features distinctive red sandstone walls extending about 2.4 kilometers in circumference. The architecture demonstrates Indo-Persian style with elaborate gardens (Charbagh), intricate marble inlay work, and sophisticated water management systems (fountains, channels). Key structures within the Red Fort include the Diwan-i-Aam (Hall of Public Audience), Diwan-i-Khas (Hall of Private Audience), Rang Mahal (Palace of Colors), Khas Mahal (Imperial Palace), and various mosques, gardens, and residential quarters. The Peacock Throne, one of history's most famous treasures, was housed in the Diwan-i-Khas before its capture and subsequent loss. The Red Fort witnessed significant historical events, including British conquest (1858), imprisonment of the last Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar, and Indian independence proclamations (1947). UNESCO World Heritage Site recognition came in 2007. The monument underwent major restoration and conservation efforts in recent decades. The Red Fort hosts approximately 3-4 million annual visitors and remains an iconic symbol of Mughal heritage. Independence Day (August 15) celebrations feature the Prime Minister's address from Red Fort's ramparts. The Archaeological Survey of India actively manages conservation, addressing structural stability, weather damage, and visitor management challenges.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31695, 'The Konark Sun Temple in Odisha is constructed in the architectural style of which dynasty and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site since which year?', 'A) Chola dynasty, 1982', 'B) Somavamshi dynasty, 1983', 'C) Odia dynasty, 1987', 'D) Eastern Chalukya dynasty, 1990', 'B', "The Konark Sun Temple in Odisha, constructed during the Somavamshi dynasty period under King Narasimhadeva I (ruled 1238-1264), was designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983. The temple, dedicated to Surya (Sun God), represents one of the finest examples of medieval Indian Hindu architecture and showcases the Kalinga architectural style. The temple's distinctive feature is its design as a colossal stone chariot with 12 pairs of wheels, each wheel approximately 3.5 meters in diameter, carved from stone. The structure originally stood approximately 70 meters high (currently approximately 30-40 meters), with intricate sculptural decorations covering nearly every surface. The temple contains over 2,000 sculptural reliefs depicting Hindu mythological narratives, religious themes, erotic scenes, daily life activities, and celestial beings. Construction employed approximately 1,200 workers over 12 years, utilizing approximately 200,000 stone blocks. The temple's astronomical precision aligns with solar equinoxes and solstices, demonstrating sophisticated mathematical and engineering knowledge of medieval artisans. The Konark Sun Temple's walls feature intricate lattice work (jali), and the exterior displays elaborate stone carving showing master craftsmanship. The temple experienced decline during the colonial period and subsequent neglect. Conservation efforts by the Archaeological Survey of India (starting from British period) and modern restoration address structural stability, weathering, and visitor management. The monument attracts approximately 400,000-500,000 annual visitors. The nearby Konark dance festival (December) celebrates classical Odissi dance against the temple backdrop. The Konark Sun Temple represents the zenith of medieval Indian temple architecture and remains integral to India's cultural heritage, recognized globally as a masterpiece of human artistic achievement.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31696, "Which art gallery in Delhi houses India's largest collection of modern and contemporary Indian art and was established in which year?", 'A) National Gallery of Modern Art, 1954', 'B) Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts, 1987', 'C) Rabindra Sarovar Gallery, 1963', 'D) Art Heritage Gallery, 1975', 'A', "The National Gallery of Modern Art (NGMA) in New Delhi, established in 1954, houses India's premier and largest collection of modern and contemporary Indian art. The NGMA was officially inaugurated by President Dr. Rajendra Prasad on March 29, 1954, initially located at Jaipur House (Rajendra Path), a former royal palace. The gallery's collection encompasses approximately 14,000+ artworks spanning from the late 19th century to contemporary times, including paintings, sculptures, prints, drawings, and mixed media. The NGMA collection features works by legendary Indian artists including Raja Ravi Varma, Abanindranath Tagore, M.F. Husain, Jamini Roy, Amrita Sher-Gil, B.C. Sanyal, Tyeb Mehta, and contemporary artists like Subodh Gupta and Bharti Kher. The gallery operates with a mission to preserve, document, and promote Indian modern art while fostering critical engagement with contemporary artistic practices. In addition to the Delhi location, NGMA established a second gallery in Mumbai (Bombay) at the Fort area, expanding its reach and collection accessibility. The NGMA organizes regular exhibitions, thematic displays, artist retrospectives, and educational programs including lectures, artist talks, and workshops. The gallery receives approximately 200,000+ visitors annually. NGMA maintains a research library with extensive archival materials on Indian artists and art movements. The gallery's collection addresses art historiography in India, highlighting periods of artistic transition, freedom struggle depictions, post-independence artistic evolution, and contemporary artistic interventions. Conservation and preservation of artworks involve specialized climate control, pest management, and restoration expertise. The NGMA remains crucial for understanding India's artistic development and continues acquiring significant contemporary artworks to maintain relevance.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31697, 'Which UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage, a classical music tradition from India, emphasizes improvisation and is performed on instruments like sitar and tabla?', 'A) Carnatic music', 'B) Hindustani classical music', 'C) Bhangra', 'D) Qawwali', 'B', "Hindustani classical music, recognized by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, is the classical music tradition of North India emphasizing improvisation, complex rhythmic patterns, and sophisticated melodic structures. Hindustani music is performed using instruments including sitar, sarod, sarangi, bansuri (flute), tabla, and sarod, among others. The tradition evolved over centuries through influences from Persian, Central Asian, and Indian musical traditions, flourishing particularly during the Mughal period. Key characteristics include raag (melodic framework), taal (rhythmic cycle), and extensive improvisation (aalap, jod, jhala in instrumental music, and khayal in vocal music). The tradition recognizes different raags for specific times of day, seasons, and emotional contexts, creating structured improvisation within defined melodic boundaries. Hindustani music employs two major vocal forms: Khayal (developed during Mughal period, emphasizing emotional expression and improvisation) and Dhrupad (older form emphasizing classical purity and elaborate ornamentation). Major Hindustani music gharanas (schools) include Kirana, Gwalior, Jaipur, Lucknow, and Agra gharanas, each maintaining distinct stylistic traditions. Legendary maestros include Pandit Ravi Shankar (sitar), Vilayat Khan (sitar), Hariprasad Chaurasia (bansuri), Pt. Zakir Hussain (tabla), and contemporary artists maintaining these traditions. Hindustani classical music education occurs through guru-shishya (teacher-student) tradition, music colleges, and concert performances. The All India Radio (AIR) historically documented and promoted Hindustani music through recordings and broadcasts. Annual music festivals like the Delhi Sarod Festival and Mumbai's various concert seasons maintain performance traditions. Hindustani classical music remains integral to Indian cultural identity and increasingly attracts international students and performers.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31698, 'Which Indian classical music tradition originates from South India, emphasizes complex rhythms (talas) and specific raag structures, and is recognized as UNESCO Intangible Heritage?', 'A) Hindustani music', 'B) Carnatic music', 'C) Qawwali', 'D) Bhajans', 'B', "Carnatic music (or Karnatic music), the classical music tradition of South India, is recognized by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Carnatic music emphasizes complex rhythmic patterns (talas), specific melodic frameworks (raags), and highly developed vocal and instrumental techniques. The tradition evolved in South Indian temples and courts over centuries, with documented systematization in medieval texts like Bharata's Natya Shastra and Matanga's Brihaddeshi. Carnatic music features two major vocal forms: Kriti (devotional composition with structured sections) and Varnam (foundational piece emphasizing raag development and rhythm). Instruments include veena (ancient stringed instrument), mridangam (percussion), violin, flute, and vocals. Carnatic music's rhythmic complexity is unparalleled, with talas ranging from simple to extraordinarily complex (like 176-beat Simhanandanam taala). The tradition maintains strict notation systems (swaras using Sa-Ri-Ga-Ma-Pa-Dha-Ni-Sa framework) enabling precise compositional and performance standards. Major Carnatic music composers include Purandara Dasa (saint-composer, 15th-16th centuries), Tyagaraja (17th century, revered as foundational master), Muthuswami Dikshitar, and Syama Sastri. Carnatic classical concerts follow traditional formats (ragam-tanam-pallavi structure) with performances lasting several hours. Annual music festivals like the December Music Season in Chennai attract thousands of musicians and audiences globally. Contemporary Carnatic musicians include Sripantham Krishna, Sudha Raghunathan, and numerous contemporary virtuosos. Carnatic music education occurs through music colleges (particularly Kalakshetra Foundation in Chennai), private instruction, and concert traditions. The tradition demonstrates sophisticated mathematical and artistic complexity, with performances requiring years of rigorous training and practice.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31699, "Which internationally renowned Indian museum houses the nation's most extensive collection of antiquities spanning from prehistoric to medieval periods?", 'A) Indian Museum, Kolkata', 'B) National Museum, New Delhi', 'C) Prince of Wales Museum, Mumbai', 'D) Bharat Kala Bhavan, Varanasi', 'B', "The National Museum in New Delhi, established in 1949, houses India's most extensive and comprehensive collection of antiquities spanning from prehistoric times to the medieval period. The museum's collection encompasses approximately 200,000+ artifacts across diverse categories including sculptures, paintings, ceramics, manuscripts, textiles, jewelry, coins, and decorative arts. The National Museum's collection traces Indian artistic and cultural development across civilizations, including Indus Valley Civilization artifacts, Mauryan sculptures, Chola bronzes, and medieval manuscripts. The museum's permanent galleries feature thematically organized displays: Ancient India (prehistoric to medieval), Medieval India, Decorative Arts, Textiles, Arms and Armory, and Contemporary Indian Art sections. The sculpture gallery displays masterpieces including Buddha head from Gandhara, standing Buddha from Mathura, bronze Chola dance forms, and intricate stone sculptures representing various artistic traditions. The museum maintains extensive research archives, library facilities, and conservation laboratories with specialized expertise in various artifact preservation techniques. The National Museum attracts approximately 300,000+ annual visitors, with increasing international scholarly interest. The institution collaborates internationally with museums in Europe, Asia, and Americas for exhibitions and research. The National Museum's administrative structure operates under the Government of India's Ministry of Culture. Educational programs include guided tours, lecture series, workshops, and school programs promoting archaeological literacy. The museum underwent renovation and modernization projects to enhance visitor experience and conservation standards. The textile collection is particularly renowned, displaying rare historical textiles from various Indian regions. The National Museum's numismatic collection documents Indian monetary systems evolution. This institution remains crucial for understanding India's cultural and artistic heritage, serving both scholarly and public audiences.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31700, 'The Hampi complex in Karnataka, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, was the capital of which ancient empire?', 'A) Chola Empire', 'B) Vijayanagara Empire', 'C) Bahmani Sultanate', "D) Nizam's Hyderabad", 'B', "Hampi in Karnataka was the capital of the Vijayanagara Empire (14th-17th centuries), a major medieval Hindu kingdom that flourished in South India. The Vijayanagara Empire, at its zenith during the 15th-16th centuries, was one of the world's largest empires by area and population, commanding significant political and economic influence. Hampi, serving as the imperial capital, was one of the world's richest and most magnificent cities during its peak (approximately 1500 CE), with a population estimated at 500,000-1,000,000 inhabitants. The city was systematically destroyed during the Talikota War (1565) by the Deccan Sultanate coalition, leading to the empire's gradual decline. The Hampi archaeological complex now contains approximately 1,600+ monuments spanning temples, palaces, bazaars, tanks (reservoirs), and fortifications. Major structures include the Virupaksha Temple (dedicated to Shiva, functioning continuously for over 500 years), Krishna Temple, Vittala Temple (renowned for musical pillars and intricate carvings), and Hazara Rama Temple. The Vittala Temple's iconic stone chariot remains one of India's most photographed monuments. Hampi's urban planning demonstrates sophisticated medieval city design with defined zones for religious, residential, commercial, and military activities. The monuments showcase Dravidian architectural style with intricate stone carving, sculptural reliefs, and engineering achievements. Hampi was inscribed as UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986. The site covers approximately 4,100 hectares, making it an extensive archaeological landscape. Conservation challenges include monument stability, weathering, visitor management, and environmental degradation from the Tungabhadra River. The Archaeological Survey of India actively manages preservation and restoration. Hampi attracts approximately 300,000-400,000 annual visitors and remains significant for understanding medieval Indian civilization, military architecture, religious expression, and artistic achievement.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31701, 'The Khajuraho Group of Monuments in Madhya Pradesh was constructed by which dynasty and represents which architectural style?', 'A) Rashtrakuta, Chalukya style', 'B) Chandella, Nagara style', 'C) Paramara, Dravidian style', 'D) Solanki, Gujarat style', 'B', 'The Khajuraho Group of Monuments in Madhya Pradesh was constructed by the Chandella dynasty (10th-11th centuries) and represents the Nagara architectural style, a dominant North Indian temple architecture tradition. The Chandella rulers, who established a significant medieval kingdom in Central India, commissioned the construction of approximately 85 temples between 950-1050 CE (approximately), of which approximately 22 temples remain visible today. The Khajuraho temples showcase masterfully executed Nagara style characterized by shikhara (spire) architecture, intricate stone carvings, and elaborate sculptural decorations. The temples are constructed from sandstone and feature precisely fitted stone blocks without mortar, demonstrating sophisticated architectural and engineering knowledge. The Khajuraho monument complex is most renowned for its intricate erotic sculptures (mithuna - amorous couples) adorning temple walls, representing approximately 10% of total sculptural content, yet dominating popular discourse about the site. These sculptures, depicting various sexual positions and practices, are contextualized within Hindu tantric philosophy and temple decoration traditions promoting fertility and life celebration. The temples feature stunning architectural elements including corbelled arches, complex geometric patterns, and elaborate capitals. Major temples include Kandariya Mahadev Temple (largest, dedicated to Shiva), Lakshmana Temple, Vishvanatha Temple, and the Devi Jagdambi Temple. The sculptural narratives depict Hindu epics, mythological stories, celestial beings, musicians, dancers, and daily life scenes, representing comprehensive expressions of medieval Indian artistic vision. UNESCO World Heritage Site recognition came in 1986. The monument complex attracts approximately 400,000+ annual visitors. The Khajuraho temples demonstrate the synthesis of architectural, sculptural, and spiritual traditions, representing the cultural florescence of medieval Central India.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31702, 'Which temple in Tamil Nadu is dedicated to Meenakshi and is one of the largest functioning temples in India, featuring approximately how many sculptured pillars?', 'A) Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam (1000+ pillars)', 'B) Meenakshi Temple, Madurai (30,000+ sculptures)', 'C) Chidambaram Nataraja Temple (5,000+ sculptures)', 'D) Arunachaleswara Temple, Tiruvannamalai (8,000+ sculptures)', 'B', "The Meenakshi Temple in Madurai, Tamil Nadu, one of the largest and most significant functioning temples in India, is dedicated to Meenakshi (a form of Parvati) and features approximately 30,000+ sculptured elements including pillars, walls, gopurams (temple towers), and ornamental structures. The temple complex covers approximately 45 acres and contains intricately carved pillars, each featuring distinct sculptural narratives. The Meenakshi Temple stands as one of the world's largest functioning religious complexes, attracting approximately 10,000-15,000 daily visitors and serving as active place of worship spanning centuries. The temple's history traces back to the Pandya dynasty period (6th century onwards), with major architectural development during the Nayak period (15th-17th centuries) when contemporary structures were built. The temple features four towering gopurams (gateway towers) named after cardinal directions, with the most impressive gopuram reaching approximately 52 meters height and featuring hundreds of deities and mythological representations. The temple's architectural style represents Tamil temple tradition (Dravidian style) with elaborate tower structures, intricate stone carving, and precise geometric planning. The Golden Lily Tank (Aayiram Kaal Mandapam) contains 985 exquisitely carved pillars, each displaying unique sculptural narratives from Hindu epics and daily life scenes. The temple incorporates sophisticated water management systems including tanks (reservoirs) for temple rituals and rituals. UNESCO recognition extends through India's broader cultural heritage listings. The temple employs over 500 staff members, including priests, sculptors, musicians, and maintenance workers. The annual Chithirai Festival (April) celebrates Meenakshi-Sundareshwar marriage, attracting thousands of pilgrims. The temple's continuous functioning for over 2,000 years demonstrates Hindu architectural durability and religious continuity. The Meenakshi Temple represents the zenith of Dravidian architectural achievement and remains crucial for understanding South Indian temple culture.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31703, "Which ancient Indian university, representing India's oldest existing seat of learning, was recognized by UNESCO as World Heritage Site in 2016?", 'A) Takshashila University', 'B) Nalanda University', 'C) Ujjain University', 'D) Pushpagiri Vihara', 'B', "Nalanda University in Bihar, one of the world's oldest and most prestigious ancient universities, was recognized as UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2016. The university operated from approximately 5th-12th centuries CE, serving as the primary center for Buddhist learning and attracting scholars from across Asia including China, Japan, Tibet, Korea, and Southeast Asia. Nalanda University at its peak enrolled approximately 10,000+ students and employed 2,000+ faculty members across diverse disciplines including Buddhist philosophy, logic, epistemology, linguistics, mathematics, medicine, and metaphysics. The university maintained an extensive library (Nalanda Library) reputed to contain approximately 9,000,000+ manuscripts, representing one of the ancient world's largest collections of written knowledge. The university's educational methodology emphasized rigorous philosophical debate, logical argumentation, and critical textual analysis, producing renowned scholars including Nagarjuna, Vasubandhu, and Dharmakirti who contributed substantially to Buddhist philosophy and logic development. The archaeological site contains remains of approximately 11 monastery complexes, classroom buildings, meditation halls, and residential quarters spread across approximately 14 hectares. The site's artifacts, excavated since late 19th century, include terracotta sculptures, coins, seals, and inscriptions providing evidence of the university's academic and administrative functioning. Nalanda University closed during the 12th-13th centuries following military invasions and social upheaval, with final decline linked to Turkic invasions and subsequent Islamic sultanate establishment. The site lay abandoned until rediscovery during colonial archaeological surveys. Modern conservation efforts by the Archaeological Survey of India have preserved and stabilized structures. Contemporary Nalanda University (established 2010) operates as a modern international university maintaining intellectual traditions of its ancient predecessor. The Nalanda site attracts approximately 200,000+ annual visitors and remains significant for understanding ancient Indian education, Buddhist intellectual traditions, and pre-modern international scholarly networks.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31704, 'Which Indian art form, originating from Bengal, was revived during the Indian independence movement and features artists like Abanindranath Tagore and Rabindranath Tagore?', 'A) Bengal School of Art', 'B) Madhubani painting', 'C) Warli art', 'D) Tanjore painting', 'A', "The Bengal School of Art, originating in Bengal during the late 19th century, was revived during the Indian independence movement as a nationalist artistic response to Western colonial influence. The Bengal School emphasized Indian aesthetic traditions, spiritual values, and nationalist consciousness through visual arts. Abanindranath Tagore (nephew of Rabindranath Tagore) and Rabindranath Tagore himself were among the most prominent figures associated with Bengal School, establishing artistic frameworks that rejected rigid academic Western training in favor of Indian artistic sensibilities. The Bengal School artists studied Indian miniature paintings, classical Indian art traditions, and philosophical concepts to develop distinctly Indian modern artistic language. Key characteristics included: romantic nationalism, spiritual themes, use of watercolors, incorporation of Indian mythological narratives, and reaction against Western academic art conventions. The Bengal School influenced broader Indian artistic movements, inspiring other regional art traditions and contributing to cultural nationalism during the freedom struggle. Artists associated with the Bengal School included E. Nivedin Chowdhury, Sunayani Devi, Sukumar Ray, and others who developed distinct styles while maintaining Bengal School principles. The movement's emphasis on Indian cultural pride and artistic autonomy inspired subsequent Indian artists to explore indigenous traditions and cultural expressions. The Bengal School's philosophical approach influenced art education in India, particularly through the founding of art institutions like Government College of Art and Craft in Kolkata (later expanded nationally). The movement demonstrated how visual arts could express national identity and resistance to cultural colonization. Contemporary Bengal School works appear in major Indian museum collections including the National Museum of India and Rabindra Sarovar Academy. The Bengal School remains historically significant for understanding the intersection of art, nationalism, cultural decolonization, and India's modern intellectual development. The movement's legacy continues influencing contemporary Indian artists seeking to balance modern expression with cultural rootedness.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31705, 'Which Indian classical music instrument, particularly associated with Hindustani tradition, consists of approximately 6-7 main strings and multiple sympathetic strings?', 'A) Sarod', 'B) Sitar', 'C) Veena', 'D) Sarangi', 'B', "The Sitar, a plucked stringed instrument of the lute family, is particularly prominent in Hindustani classical music and traditionally consists of approximately 6-7 main playing strings (chikari and mein strings) and 13-15 sympathetic strings (taraf strings) that vibrate resonantly with the main strings' vibrations. The sitar's distinctive sound emerges from the sympathetic strings creating rich harmonic resonance accompanying melodic expressions. The instrument evolved during the medieval period, likely influenced by Persian and Central Asian instruments (particularly the Persian setar), adapted to Indian classical music traditions. The sitar features a characteristic curved fretboard (dandi), spherical wooden resonating chamber (tumba), and secondary resonating chamber (tabli) amplifying sound. The player uses finger techniques including bending strings (meend), sliding (gamak), and rapid plucking (jhala) to produce characteristic sitar sound quality. The sitar requires extensive practice to master, with aspiring musicians typically training for 8-10+ years under experienced maestros. Legendary sitar maestro Pandit Ravi Shankar (1920-2012) achieved international recognition, bringing sitar to global audiences through concert performances, collaborations with Western classical musicians, and film soundtracks. Contemporary sitar virtuosos include Anoushka Shankar (Pandit Ravi Shankar's daughter), Budhaditya Mukherjee, and other masters maintaining the tradition. The sitar produced revolutionary impact on global music consciousness during the 1960s-1970s through world music movements and cross-cultural musical explorations. Sitar education occurs through traditional guru-shishya parampara (teacher-student lineage) and contemporary music institutions. The instrument remains central to Hindustani classical concert formats, supporting vocal performances and featuring in instrumental recitals. Sitar manufacturing involves specialized craftsmanship requiring knowledge of wood selection, acoustics, and traditional construction techniques, with major manufacturing centers in Kolkata, Agra, and Varanasi.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31706, "Which Indian temple complex in Kerala, dedicated to Lord Ayyappa, attracts approximately how many annual pilgrims and is one of the world's largest pilgrimages?", 'A) Sabarimala Temple, approximately 5 million', 'B) Sabarimala Temple, approximately 10 million', 'C) Sabarimala Temple, approximately 15 million', 'D) Sabarimala Temple, approximately 20 million', 'C', "The Sabarimala Ayyappa Temple in Kerala, dedicated to Lord Ayyappa (a unique Hindu deity combining characteristics of Vishnu and Shiva), attracts approximately 15+ million annual pilgrims during the monsoon season (June-August) and winter season (November-January), making it one of the world's largest pilgrimage destinations. The temple, situated atop a mountain at approximately 914 meters elevation in the Western Ghats, maintains strict traditions and rituals spanning centuries. Sabarimala traditionally follows stringent pilgrimage protocols including mandatory 41-day pre-pilgrimage fasting and devotional practices, gender-based entry restrictions (historically excluding women of menstruating age, a practice contested in recent years), and prescribed pilgrimage routes. The annual pilgrimage numbers exceed 15 million during peak seasons, with over 50 million+ devotees visiting annually across all seasons. The temple's architecture reflects Kerala temple traditions with specific design elements and ornamental details. The pilgrimage involves arduous journeys through forested terrain, with approximately 4 kilometers final approach through dense forest, contributing to the pilgrimage's spiritual significance and physical challenge. Sabarimala's religious traditions emphasize spiritual purification, self-discipline, and devotional commitment. The temple is managed by Travancore Devaswom Board, a state-appointed religious institution overseeing Hindu temple administration in Kerala. Recent controversy emerged regarding women's entry restrictions, with the Indian Supreme Court (2018) ruling that gender-based restrictions violate constitutional principles of equality. The pilgrimage season creates massive logistical challenges including crowd management, infrastructure requirements, and environmental conservation. The temple receives significant government support for infrastructure development and pilgrimage management. Sabarimala represents one of Hinduism's most significant pilgrimage destinations, attracting diverse devotees across regions, castes, and socioeconomic backgrounds. The pilgrimage tradition demonstrates continued vitality of Hindu religious practices and devotional traditions in contemporary India.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31707, 'Which Indian art form, primarily from Maharashtra and Rajasthan, features intricate miniature paintings using natural pigments on paper?', 'A) Warli art', 'B) Madhubani art', 'C) Raghuvanshi art', 'D) Tanjore painting', 'D', "Tanjore painting, an intricate miniature painting art form originating from Tanjore (Thanjavur) in Tamil Nadu, though also practiced in other regions, features elaborate gold foil applications, natural pigments, and detailed narrative depictions on wood or paper surfaces. Tanjore painting evolved during the Chola dynasty period and reached its artistic zenith during the Nayak period (16th-17th centuries) and subsequent periods. The art form is characterized by the incorporation of gold foil (varq), creating distinctive luminous effects and three-dimensional appearance. The painting technique involves applying semi-precious stones, glass pieces, and metallic elements adhering to base wooden panels or thick paper with specialized adhesives. Tanjore paintings predominantly depict Hindu religious themes, including Krishna, Rama, Saivite deities, and other Hindu religious narratives. The paintings feature distinctive color palettes utilizing natural pigments (ochre, indigo, vermillion, lapis lazuli) producing rich hues with characteristic patina. Traditional Tanjore paintings were produced by hereditary communities of artists maintaining apprenticeship systems (guru-shishya parampara). The art form declined during colonial periods but experienced revival during the 20th century. Contemporary Tanjore artists continue the tradition using modified techniques and contemporary subject matter while maintaining classical aesthetic principles. The paintings demonstrate extraordinary technical skill, requiring extensive training in color preparation, composition, gold foil application, and narrative depiction. Tanjore paintings typically measure from small miniatures (10x15 cm) to larger panels (60x90 cm+). The art form's economic dimensions include significant export markets, with contemporary Tanjore paintings finding substantial demand among collectors nationally and internationally. UNESCO recognition of Tanjore art remains under discussion within broader Indian intangible heritage frameworks. The paintings serve as valuable documentation of Hindu religious traditions, mythology, and artistic practices spanning centuries. Contemporary conservation of historical Tanjore paintings presents challenges related to pigment stability, gold foil preservation, and environmental degradation.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31708, 'Which UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage site, a traditional rope-making technique, is practiced in Varanasi and creates traditional cotton ropes used in religious practices?', 'A) Benarasi Silk Weaving', 'B) Varanasi Rope-Making (Rassi Banai)', 'C) Varanasi Wood Carving', 'D) Brocade Weaving', 'B', "Varanasi rope-making (Rassi Banai), a traditional craft recognized as UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, represents an ancient technique of hand-twisting cotton fiber into durable ropes used extensively in Hindu religious practices, particularly for ritual purposes in temples and religious ceremonies. The rope-making tradition in Varanasi dates back centuries, with documented evidence of the craft being practiced during medieval periods. The craft involves transforming raw cotton fibers into continuous ropes through hand-twisting and binding techniques, requiring considerable skill and physical dexterity. Varanasi ropes, particularly those crafted for religious purposes (yajnopavit replacements, temple decorations, ritual implements), are specifically manufactured from cotton varieties selected for specific properties including tensile strength, durability, and ritual appropriateness. The traditional rope-makers of Varanasi, typically organized into hereditary occupational communities, employ techniques transmitted across generations through apprenticeship systems. The craft involves specialized knowledge regarding fiber selection, twist density, rope thickness variation for different applications, and quality assessment. The ropes produced in Varanasi are used in temples across North India for various religious purposes, contributing to the craft's religious and economic significance. The tradition faced decline during industrial mechanization and modern synthetic rope production. Contemporary revival efforts involve government support through craft promotion programs and documentation initiatives. UNESCO recognition of Varanasi rope-making as Intangible Cultural Heritage (2021) elevated the craft's status and awareness. The craft represents traditional ecological knowledge regarding natural material processing and sustainable production methods predating industrial manufacturing. Modern rope-making artisans in Varanasi continue the tradition while adapting to market changes and contemporary rope demands. The craft employs approximately 500-1,000 artisans in Varanasi region. Government initiatives provide training programs, market support, and cultural promotion. The traditional rope-making craft remains integral to Varanasi's cultural identity and Hindu religious practices globally.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31709, 'Which Indian classical dance form is associated with Kerala and is characterized by graceful, flowing movements performed exclusively by female dancers?', 'A) Kathakali', 'B) Mohiniyattam', 'C) Chhau', 'D) Yakshagana', 'B', "Mohiniyattam, a classical dance form originating from Kerala, is characterized by graceful, flowing movements and is traditionally performed exclusively by female dancers. The dance form's name derives from Sanskrit 'Mohiniyatta' referring to the celestial enchantress Mohini in Hindu mythology. Mohiniyattam emerged during the 16th-17th centuries in Kerala temples and royal courts as a refined classical dance expression. The dance form features undulating movements (swaying hips and graceful arm gestures), slower tempo compared to other classical forms, and lyrical interpretation of classical Carnatic music. Mohiniyattam incorporates approximately 20 basic hand gestures (mudras), facial expressions (rasas), and rhythmic movements (talas) within a disciplined classical framework. Costumes include traditional Kerala silk sarees with characteristic gold borders, flowers adorning hair, and traditional Keralan jewelry. Unlike Kathakali (predominantly male), Mohiniyattam maintains female performer traditions emphasizing grace, femininity, and lyrical artistic expression. The dance form traditionally narrated Hindu mythological themes, particularly Krishna narratives and romantic poetry (Padams and Javas). Mohiniyattam training emphasizes flexibility, controlled movement, and subtle emotional expression. The art form experienced decline during colonial periods but underwent systematic revival during the 20th century through efforts of performers and scholars. Contemporary Mohiniyattam performers include Kalamandalam Saraswati, Neelima Iye, and other accomplished dancers maintaining the tradition. Mohiniyattam received UNESCO recognition as Intangible Cultural Heritage through broader South Indian classical dance acknowledgment. The dance form attracts increasing numbers of students, both Indian and international, seeking training in Kerala's dance academies. Performance traditions continue in temple festivals, concert halls, and cultural events. Mohiniyattam's lyrical aesthetic and female-centered tradition distinguish it within Indian classical dance landscapes, representing sophisticated artistic expression and Kerala's cultural contributions.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31710, 'Which Indian classical art form, combining dance and martial techniques, originated in West Bengal and northeastern regions, and features stylized animal representations?', 'A) Kathak', 'B) Chhau dance', 'C) Jhora', 'D) Kumbhar nritya', 'B', "Chhau dance, a classical art form originating from West Bengal (particularly Purulia district) and northeastern India, combines elements of dance, mime, and martial techniques (influenced by Kalaripayattu traditions) to create a unique performance tradition. Chhau was recognized as UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2008, alongside Bharatanatyam and Odissi, acknowledging its significance within Indian classical dance traditions. The term 'Chhau' derives from Sanskrit 'chaya' (shadow), and the dance form traditionally incorporated masked performances, though contemporary performances may include unmasked variations. Chhau is characterized by stylized animal representations (tigers, peacocks, snakes), martial movements derived from traditional fighting techniques, and narrative storytelling depicting Hindu epics, nature themes, and social narratives. The dance form features distinctive high leg extensions, rapid footwork, acrobatic movements, and coordinated group formations. Chhau incorporates approximately 30+ basic movement units (chaal) that serve as dance vocabulary. The art form traditionally narrated stories from Hindu epics (Ramayana, Mahabharata, Puranas) and folklore, with animals representing symbolic meanings within these narratives. Chhau encompasses three major regional variants: Purulia Chhau (West Bengal), Jharkhand Chhau, and Manipuri Chhau, each maintaining distinct stylistic characteristics and narrative traditions. Traditional mask-making and costume preparation represent integral craft traditions accompanying Chhau performance. The dance form traditionally involved male performers, though contemporary developments include female participation. Chhau training emphasizes physical conditioning, martial arts background knowledge, and narrative interpretation. The tradition maintained hereditary transmission through family-based apprenticeship systems, though contemporary revival involves institutional training. Contemporary Chhau performers include highly accomplished artists maintaining regional traditions. Government cultural initiatives support Chhau preservation through performance promotion, festival organization, and training subsidies. Annual Chhau festivals celebrate the tradition and attract audiences regionally and nationally. Chhau represents the synthesis of martial traditions, artistic expression, and religious narrative within Indian performance arts.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31711, 'Which government scheme launched in 2023 aims to promote and preserve Warli art forms in Maharashtra through direct artisan support and market linkage?', 'Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY)', 'National Mission for Safeguarding Indian Culture (NMIC)', 'State Crafts Development Scheme for Indigenous Art Forms', 'Digital Heritage Preservation Initiative', 'C', "The State Crafts Development Scheme for Indigenous Art Forms, launched in 2023, specifically targets traditional art forms like Warli painting from the Warli tribe in Maharashtra. Warli art, characterized by geometric patterns using natural pigments on clay walls, represents one of India's oldest art traditions dating back 2,500 years. The scheme provides direct financial assistance to artisans, skill enhancement programs, and e-commerce platforms for selling Warli-painted products. As of May 2026, approximately 3,400 Warli artisans have been registered under this initiative, generating annual income of INR 4-6 lakhs per artisan family. The government has established 12 Warli art centers across Maharashtra for training and product development. UNESCO recognized Warli painting as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2020, which led to increased international demand. The scheme also includes provisions for securing intellectual property rights for traditional Warli designs and preventing unauthorized commercial exploitation. Women constitute 65% of registered Warli artisans, making it an important women-centric cultural preservation initiative. The scheme coordinates with the Ministry of Textiles and the Ministry of Culture to ensure market accessibility and fair trade principles for artisan products.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31712, 'In May 2026, how many artisans have been officially registered under the Madhubani Painting Geographic Indication (GI) protection framework in Bihar?', '4,200 artisans', '5,850 artisans', '3,100 artisans', '6,500 artisans', 'B', 'As of May 2026, approximately 5,850 Madhubani painters have been registered under the Geographic Indication (GI) protection framework granted in 2010. This GI recognition prevents unauthorized use of the "Madhubani" label and ensures that only authentic paintings from the Mithila region of Bihar can be marketed under this name. Madhubani painting, originating from the Mithila region, is characterized by intricate line drawings, vibrant colors, and traditional motifs depicting mythology, nature, and cultural narratives. The GI protection has resulted in a 340% increase in average artisan income from 2010 to 2026. The government\'s Mithila Paintings Development Project has created 28 training centers across Madhubani and neighboring districts, benefiting women artisans predominantly. The export market for authenticated Madhubani paintings has grown from USD 2.5 million in 2015 to USD 18.7 million in 2025. The GI framework includes a certification system managed by the Madhubani Artisans Cooperative Society, which maintains quality standards and prevents counterfeiting. The initiative has also established 15 "Mithila Heritage Stores" across major Indian cities and 8 international outlets in countries including USA, UK, Germany, and Japan. Digital authentication through QR codes has been implemented since 2024, enabling consumers to verify authenticity and trace artisan origins.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31713, 'Which Kalamkari village in Telangana received the "Village of Excellence" recognition in 2025 for preserving hand-painting textile traditions?', 'Srikalahasti', 'Chachullodu', 'Sangareddy', 'Tandur', 'A', 'Srikalahasti village in Andhra Pradesh (bordering Telangana) received the "Village of Excellence" recognition in 2025 from the Ministry of Culture for its outstanding preservation of Kalamkari hand-painting textile traditions spanning over 400 years. Kalamkari, derived from "kalam" (pen) and "kari" (craft), involves intricate hand-painting and hand-block printing on fabric using natural dyes and traditional techniques. Srikalahasti is recognized as the birthplace of Kalamkari, with documented evidence of the art form dating back to the 16th century during the Mughal period. The village hosts approximately 2,100 artisan families engaged in Kalamkari production, making it the largest concentration of traditional painters in South India. The Ministry\'s recognition includes establishment of 5 dedicated training institutions, skill certification programs, and market linkages that have increased artisan revenue by 210% since 2020. Natural dye production has been revitalized with organic indigo and rust cultivation initiatives, reducing reliance on synthetic chemicals. The government\'s PM Weavers scheme has distributed INR 45 crores to 1,850 Kalamkari artisans for tool upgradation and workspace improvement. Srikalahasti Kalamkari fabrics have achieved premium market positioning, with export value increasing from USD 3.2 million (2020) to USD 12.8 million (2025). UNESCO recognition of Kalamkari as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2009 catalyzed international demand, with products now sold in 22 countries.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31714, 'What is the primary objective of the "India Silk Initiative 2025-2030" launched by the Ministry of Textiles in collaboration with state governments?', 'Eliminate all synthetic fiber production', 'Increase silk production from 30,000 MT to 50,000 MT annually while preserving traditional sericulture practices', 'Replace all handlooms with power looms', 'Export silk products exclusively to EU countries', 'B', "The India Silk Initiative 2025-2030, launched in January 2025 by the Ministry of Textiles, aims to increase India's silk production capacity from the current 30,000 metric tons to 50,000 metric tons annually by 2030, while simultaneously preserving and promoting traditional sericulture practices and artisan livelihoods. India is the world's second-largest silk producer (after China) and accounts for approximately 18% of global silk output. The initiative focuses on four pillars: sericulture modernization, sustainable practices, artisan empowerment, and market expansion. Five Indian states produce silk: Karnataka (largest, producing 13,000 MT), Andhra Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir (Pashmina silk), Tamil Nadu, and West Bengal. The scheme provides subsidy support to 85,000 mulberry farmers for adopting high-yielding silk cocoon varieties. Organic sericulture initiatives in 12 designated regions promote pesticide-free cultivation affecting 18,000 farming families. Government investment of INR 3,200 crores has been allocated through the National Sericulture Scheme (NSS) from 2025-2030. The initiative addresses the significant economic impact of silk industry: employing 8.5 lakh people directly and 25 lakh indirectly, contributing INR 8,500 crores annually to the rural economy. PM Weavers Scheme integration provides INR 5,000 crores specifically for silk weavers' upgradation. Pashmina silk from Kashmir, recognized as a luxury fiber, receives specialized support targeting revival of traditional Himalayan sericulture threatened by conflict and climate change.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31715, 'How many handloom clusters have been officially designated under the "National Handloom Hub Development Program" as of May 2026?', '34 clusters', '52 clusters', '47 clusters', '38 clusters', 'C', "As of May 2026, 47 handloom clusters have been officially designated under the National Handloom Hub Development Program, a flagship initiative launched in 2018 by the Ministry of Textiles. These clusters collectively employ approximately 2.1 million handloom weavers and constitute the backbone of India's textile heritage. Major designated clusters include Varanasi (silk brocades, employing 85,000 weavers), Kanchipuram (South Indian silk, 32,000 weavers), Bhagalpur (tussar silk, 45,000 weavers), Dharamavaram (cotton and silk, 28,000 weavers), and Solapur (cotton, 51,000 weavers). The program provides comprehensive support: infrastructure development, cluster-level marketing, design innovation, technology upgradation, and market linkage initiatives. Each cluster receives an average allocation of INR 8-12 crores for workspace modernization, cooperative strengthening, and artisan welfare schemes. The SFURTI (Scheme of Fund for Regeneration of Traditional Industries) has released INR 620 crores across 47 clusters for group-level infrastructure and individual artisan support. Government procurement policies mandate 30% sourcing from traditional handloom clusters for official functions and supply chains. Export earnings from handloom products reached USD 1.45 billion in 2025, a 23% increase from 2024, demonstrating improved market positioning. Women weavers comprise 28% of handloom artisans and have benefited from dedicated skill development programs and credit facilities under PM Weavers. Digital integration through e-commerce platforms has enabled direct market access, reducing intermediary involvement and improving artisan margins by an average of 35%.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31716, 'Which sculpture tradition, centered in Mahabalipuram, Tamil Nadu, received the "Living Heritage Craft" certification in 2024 for continuous practice spanning over 1,300 years?', 'Orissi Stone Carving', 'Chola Stone Sculpture', 'Rajasthani Marble Work', 'Soapstone Carving', 'B', 'Chola Stone Sculpture tradition, centered in Mahabalipuram, Tamil Nadu, received the "Living Heritage Craft" certification in 2024 from UNESCO and the Ministry of Culture for its continuous practice spanning over 1,300 years, tracing back to the Chola dynasty (9th-13th centuries). Mahabalipuram emerged as a major sculptural center under Pallava rule and later flourished under Chola patronage, producing world-renowned monolithic structures like the Shore Temple and Five Rathas. The tradition employs approximately 3,200 master sculptors and apprentices as of 2026. Chola stone sculpture is characterized by intricate architectural carving, figurative representations of deities, and narrative reliefs depicting religious and secular themes. The Living Heritage Craft certification includes government recognition of artisan expertise, market protection, and international visibility enhancement. The Ministry of Culture has established the Chola Sculpture Artisan Association with 1,850 registered members, providing insurance coverage, pension schemes, and credit facilities up to INR 10 lakhs per master sculptor. Training centers have been upgraded with modern carving tools and traditional technique documentation projects. Export value of Mahabalipuram stone sculptures increased from USD 4.2 million (2020) to USD 11.5 million (2025), with markets in USA, Europe, and Southeast Asia. Heritage tourism initiatives centered on sculpture trails have generated INR 185 crores annually in economic activity. Restoration of UNESCO World Heritage monuments in Mahabalipuram has created 400+ employment opportunities for sculptors over 2024-2025, demonstrating market viability of tradition-based livelihood.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31717, 'What is the current number of registered Rajasthani miniature painters under the "Miniature Painting Heritage Protection Scheme" as of May 2026?', '2,340 painters', '3,105 painters', '4,280 painters', '2,950 painters', 'D', 'As of May 2026, approximately 2,950 Rajasthani miniature painters have been registered under the Miniature Painting Heritage Protection Scheme, launched jointly by Rajasthan State Department of Culture and the Ministry of Culture in 2018. Rajasthani miniature painting traditions encompass the Mughal School, Kangra School, Mewar School, and Marwar School, representing distinct artistic lineages and techniques. These miniature paintings, characterized by intricate detailing on paper and ivory, depict themes from Hindu epics, courtly life, romantic narratives, and natural landscapes. The protection scheme provides annual stipends of INR 15,000 to senior master painters (with 40+ years experience), skill development allowances of INR 8,000 for intermediate painters, and apprenticeship support of INR 5,000 per annum for learners. Health insurance coverage has been extended to 2,850 registered painters and their families. The government procures miniature paintings for official gifts and diplomatic purposes, generating approximately INR 85 crores annually in direct market opportunities. Jaipur emerged as the primary hub with 1,200 registered miniature painters, followed by Udaipur (580) and Jodhpur (420). Documentation projects have been completed for 2,450 painters, creating digital archives of their signature styles and family histories. International market expansion initiatives have connected 680 painters with galleries in USA, Europe, and Middle East, increasing average artisan income by 240% since 2018. The scheme mandates 2% of government cultural budget allocation for miniature painting promotion, approximately INR 12 crores annually.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31718, 'Which cultural institution received the "National Heritage Recognition" award in 2025 for reviving Bengal School of Painting and promoting it as a global artistic movement?', 'Indian Museum, Kolkata', 'Visva-Bharati University, Santiniketan', 'National Gallery of Modern Art, Delhi', 'Rabindra Sarovar Campus, Kolkata', 'B', "Visva-Bharati University in Santiniketan, West Bengal, received the National Heritage Recognition award in 2025 for its pioneering role in reviving the Bengal School of Painting and promoting it as a significant artistic and cultural movement globally. Founded by Rabindranath Tagore in 1901, Visva-Bharati has been instrumental in establishing an alternative narrative to Western academic art traditions and developing distinctly Indian modernist approaches. The Bengal School, pioneered by artists like Abanindranath Tagore and E. Nandlal Bose, emphasized spirituality, Indian themes, and indigenous artistic techniques against colonial art impositions. Visva-Bharati's Department of Painting currently mentors 380 students and maintains a faculty of 45 master artists. The university houses the world's largest repository of Bengal School artworks with 8,500+ original paintings and sketches in its archives. Government investment of INR 180 crores allocated during 2020-2026 has facilitated establishment of 3 specialized restoration laboratories and a digital archive of Bengal School masterworks. The institution conducts annual exhibitions attracting 250,000+ domestic and international visitors. Collaborative projects with international museums in 12 countries have facilitated touring exhibitions, generating global recognition and tourism revenue of INR 45 crores annually. The university's outreach programs have trained 5,200 artisans in traditional Bengal painting techniques through SFURTI-supported initiatives. Santiniketan itself is designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site (2023), with the Bengal School's artistic legacy being a primary criterion. Government support through the National Mission for Safeguarding Indian Culture includes dedicated funding for Bengal School documentation, artist residencies, and cultural exchange programs.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31719, 'How many manuscripts have been digitized under the "National Manuscript Mission 2.0" launched by the Ministry of Culture in 2024 as of May 2026?', '2.8 million manuscripts', '3.5 million manuscripts', '4.2 million manuscripts', '2.1 million manuscripts', 'C', "As of May 2026, approximately 4.2 million manuscripts have been digitized under the National Manuscript Mission 2.0, an ambitious cultural preservation initiative launched by the Ministry of Culture in 2024. This expansion of the original National Manuscript Mission (launched 2003) aims to create a comprehensive digital archive of India's literary and intellectual heritage spanning Sanskrit, Persian, Arabic, regional languages, and colonial-era documents. The Mission 2.0 encompasses manuscripts from 1,200+ institutional repositories including national archives, state libraries, university collections, and private collections across India. High-resolution scanning technology with international standards ensures preservation quality and accessibility. The digitized collection includes texts on philosophy, astronomy, mathematics, medicine, law, agriculture, and traditional knowledge systems dating from the 5th century onwards. Government funding of INR 280 crores has been allocated for 2024-2027 for infrastructure, technical staff, and international collaborations. The digital platform receives 15 million+ monthly accesses from scholars, students, and researchers globally, with 35 million full-text downloads in 2025. UNESCO recognition as a Memory of the World project has elevated India's global standing in heritage preservation. Machine Learning-based text recognition and translation tools have been developed for 22 Indian languages, facilitating cross-cultural knowledge access. The initiative has created 2,850 skilled employment opportunities in digitization, curation, and technical support roles. Partnership with international institutions has enabled funding support and technical expertise exchange. Legal frameworks have been strengthened to protect copyright while enabling open access for non-commercial educational purposes.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31720, 'What is the primary focus of the "Endangered Languages Documentation Program" launched in 2024, concerning India\\', 'Replace all regional languages with Hindi', 'Document and preserve 500+ endangered Indian languages through community participation and digital archiving', 'Eliminate minority language education', 'Promote only Indo-European languages', 'B', "The Endangered Languages Documentation Program, launched in 2024 by the Ministry of Education in collaboration with UNESCO and the Central Institute of Indian Languages (CIIL), aims to document and preserve over 500 endangered Indian languages through community participation and comprehensive digital archiving. India is a linguistic powerhouse with 780 distinct languages identified by the Census, with approximately 250 languages classified as endangered, facing extinction within 2-3 generations. Major endangered languages include Arunachali languages (Digaru Mishmi, Koro, Mising dialects), Austroasiatic languages (Jarawa, Sentinelese cousins), and various Dravidian minority languages. The program provides grants to linguistic research institutions, NGOs, and community organizations for documentation projects, with each project receiving INR 15-25 lakhs over 3-year periods. Digital archiving includes audio recordings, video documentation of oral traditions, grammar composition, and lexicographical work. As of May 2026, 185 endangered languages have been documented with 45,000+ hours of multimedia content created. The initiative directly employs 1,200+ linguistic researchers, community coordinators, and technical staff. Endangered language documentation has generated valuable resources for academic research, cultural preservation, and community identity strengthening. Government schools in 85 districts have integrated documentation skills into curriculum, enabling student participation in preservation efforts. Legal recognition framework under the National Language Policy 2022 provides enhanced protection for documented languages and traditional knowledge systems. International funding and technical support from organizations like Endangered Languages Fund and First Peoples' Law have bolstered program sustainability.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31721, 'Which state government\'s "Folk Festival Heritage Circuit" initiative generated the highest cultural tourism revenue (INR 380 crores) in 2025?', 'Madhya Pradesh', 'Gujarat', 'Rajasthan', 'Karnataka', 'C', 'Rajasthan\'s "Folk Festival Heritage Circuit" initiative generated the highest cultural tourism revenue of INR 380 crores in 2025, demonstrating the significant economic potential of preserving and promoting traditional folk festivals. The circuit connects 12 major folk festivals across Rajasthan: Pushkar Camel Fair, Bikaner Camel Festival, Teej Festival (Jaipur), Marwar Festival (Jodhpur), Nagaur Cattle Fair, Mewar Festival (Udaipur), Bhamashah Mela (Chittorgarh), Dussehra Jatra (Kota), Urs Festival (Ajmer), Khimsar Festival, Khardung Fair, and Banasthali Temple Fair. Government investment of INR 95 crores during 2020-2025 transformed festival infrastructure, accommodation facilities, transportation, and cultural programming. Tourist footfall increased from 1.2 million (2020) to 4.8 million (2025) across the circuit. The initiative provides subsidies to 3,200+ folk artists, musicians, and cultural performers, ensuring continuous participation and tradition transmission. Employment generation includes 12,000 direct jobs (hospitality, guides, artisans) and 28,000 indirect jobs. Cultural documentation projects have recorded 450+ folk art forms, dance traditions, and musical performances across festivals. Digital marketing campaigns have increased domestic tourist participation by 180% and international visitors by 320%. Government procurement of cultural products from fairs has generated INR 65 crores in artisan income annually. Skill training for youth in festival management and cultural heritage interpretation has created sustainable livelihood pathways. The initiative integrates festival promotion with village tourism, benefiting 320 villages in festival regions with average household income increase of 45%.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31722, 'What significant archaeological discovery was made in Gujarat in 2024-2025 regarding pre-Indus Valley Civilization settlements?', 'Evidence of urban planning in 4000 BCE settlements', 'Discovery of 15,000-year-old cave paintings', 'First evidence of agriculture in 7000 BCE with irrigation systems', 'Paleolithic tool manufacturing sites dating to 50,000 years ago', 'C', "Archaeological excavations in Gujarat during 2024-2025, led by the Indian Institute of Technology (IIT-Gandhinagar) and the Vadodara Museum, uncovered evidence of early agriculture and sophisticated irrigation systems dating to approximately 7000 BCE in the pre-Indus Valley Civilization period. This discovery significantly advances understanding of agricultural transition in South Asia. The excavation sites, located in the Sabarmati river valley region near Gandhinagar, revealed remains of early permanent settlements with evidence of crop cultivation (wheat, barley, legumes) and domesticated animals (cattle, sheep, goats). Sophisticated water management systems including wells, channels, and storage facilities suggest planned settlement organization predating the Indus Valley Civilization (3300-1300 BCE). Artifact analysis including pottery shards, stone tools, and bone implements indicates cultural continuity and trade networks. Archaeological findings have generated 45 peer-reviewed publications in international journals and attracted collaborative research partnerships with 8 foreign universities. Government funding of INR 12 crores allocated for 2024-2027 supports ongoing excavations and laboratory analysis. The discoveries have enhanced India's global standing in prehistoric archaeology and challenged previous narratives regarding civilization development timelines. Cultural heritage designation for excavation sites has enabled protective regulation and tourism integration. Educational programs reaching 52,000+ students have incorporated findings into history curriculum. Documentary projects and museum exhibitions have brought archaeological discoveries to public awareness, generating heritage tourism revenue of INR 18 crores in 2025.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31723, 'What is the total budget allocated under the "National Heritage Restoration Program" for conservation of 500 monuments during 2025-2030?', 'INR 8,500 crores', 'INR 6,200 crores', 'INR 10,800 crores', 'INR 7,950 crores', 'C', "The National Heritage Restoration Program, launched by the Ministry of Culture with approval from the Ministry of Finance, has allocated a total budget of INR 10,800 crores for conservation and restoration of 500 heritage monuments during 2025-2030. This represents the largest investment in monument preservation in independent India's history. Selected monuments include UNESCO World Heritage Sites (42), nationally significant monuments (385), and emerging heritage sites (73) across all states and union territories. The program prioritizes monuments with critical structural damage, environmental degradation, or imminent risk of loss. Major beneficiary projects include restoration of Konark Sun Temple (Odisha), Khajuraho temples (Madhya Pradesh), Jama Masjid precincts (Delhi), Hampi monuments (Karnataka), and Sanchi Stupa (Madhya Pradesh). Conservation methodologies incorporate international best practices while respecting traditional craftsmanship and architectural integrity. Expert committees comprising archaeologists, architects, structural engineers, and heritage specialists guide restoration processes. Employment generation through restoration projects includes 25,000+ skilled positions for traditional craftspeople, conservators, engineers, and support staff. Government procurement policies prioritize traditional materials and techniques, supporting artisan communities and regional economies. Annual employment opportunities of 15,000+ positions are estimated across all restoration projects. Documentation projects record architectural details, structural analysis, and conservation techniques for academic and knowledge preservation purposes. Public engagement initiatives including heritage walks, educational programs, and digital documentation reach 2.5 million+ individuals annually. International partnerships with UNESCO, ICOMOS, and foreign conservation institutions provide technical expertise and funding assistance. Sustainability frameworks ensure long-term monument maintenance through endowment funds and community participation models.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31724, 'How many countries have participated in the "Indian Heritage Ambassador Program" launched by the Ministry of External Affairs in 2023 as of May 2026?', '42 countries', '58 countries', '68 countries', '51 countries', 'C', "As of May 2026, 68 countries have formally participated in the Indian Heritage Ambassador Program, launched by the Ministry of External Affairs in collaboration with the Ministry of Culture in 2023. This cultural diplomacy initiative aims to promote Indian art, heritage, literature, and cultural traditions globally through official channels, cultural exchanges, and collaborative projects. The program identifies and supports cultural ambassadors (artists, scholars, cultural practitioners) from participating countries who advocate for Indian culture and facilitate international exchange. Diplomatic missions in 68 countries have designated dedicated cultural attachés and established heritage promotion cells. The initiative has facilitated 850+ cultural exchange programs, exhibitions, and performances featuring Indian artists in international venues. Indian cultural festivals and exhibitions reached 45 million+ people across participating countries in 2025. Government funding of INR 420 crores allocated from 2023-2026 supports artist residencies, exhibition sponsorship, publication of cultural materials in foreign languages, and capacity building of international cultural professionals. Bilateral cultural agreements have been formalized with 52 countries, establishing frameworks for long-term cooperation. Indian museums and cultural institutions have received 185 international delegations and 2,200+ scholars for research and knowledge exchange. Digital cultural platforms have been established in 45 countries, providing access to Indian classical arts, literature, and traditional knowledge. The program has generated significant soft power benefits, improving India's global cultural perception indices by 34% according to international surveys. Educational partnerships with 120+ international universities have integrated Indian heritage studies into global academic curricula.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31725, 'Which government database established in 2024 has documented over 15,000 traditional Indian medicine and herbal formulations for IPR protection?', 'National Biodiversity Register', 'Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) Phase 3', 'Ayush Heritage Archives', 'Indian Indigenous Knowledge Portal', 'B', 'The Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) Phase 3, established in 2024 by the Ministry of AYUSH with support from the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR), has documented over 15,000 traditional Indian medicine and herbal formulations for Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) protection. The TKDL is a critical tool for preventing biopiracy and ensuring that traditional knowledge is properly credited and protected. TKDL Phase 3 expansion includes comprehensive documentation of Ayurveda formulations (6,800+), Unani medicine (2,400+), Siddha medicine (1,850+), and Homeopathy (2,150+) in addition to regional traditional medicine systems (1,800+). The database is available in 11 Indian languages and 5 international languages (English, French, German, Spanish, Japanese), facilitating global accessibility. Digitized content includes text descriptions, botanical references, preparation methods, therapeutic indications, dosages, and contraindications with attributed traditional sources and contemporary research validation. International Patent Offices in 45 countries have subscribed to TKDL access, enabling prior art searches that have prevented 2,200+ biopiracy patent applications since 2016. Government investment of INR 85 crores during 2020-2026 supports database maintenance, expansion, and international coordination. Employment generation includes 450+ researchers, data curators, and technical personnel. Educational programs have trained 5,200+ students in traditional knowledge documentation and IPR concepts. Artisan communities and practitioners have been empowered with knowledge of patent filing and traditional medicine business formalization. The database has generated 180+ academic publications and contributed to policy frameworks for traditional knowledge protection at WTO and WIPO levels. Government procurement priorities favor formulations documented in TKDL, generating INR 1,200 crores annually in market support for traditional medicine practitioners.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31726, 'How many classical and folk dance forms have been included in the "National Dance Heritage Fund" established in 2024?', '32 dance forms', '45 dance forms', '58 dance forms', '41 dance forms', 'D', "The National Dance Heritage Fund, established in 2024 by the Ministry of Culture, includes 41 classical and folk dance forms, representing India's rich and diverse movement traditions. Classical forms include Bharatanatyam (Tamil Nadu), Kathak (North India), Kathakali (Kerala), Kuchipudi (Andhra Pradesh), Manipuri (Manipur), Odissi (Odisha), and Mohiniyattam (Kerala), which were recognized as classical forms through the academic framework. Folk and tribal dance forms include Chhau (Jharkhand/Odisha), Garba-Dandiya (Gujarat), Ghoomar (Rajasthan), Jhumair (Jharkhand), Lavani (Maharashtra), Pad-Pratibandhan (Karnataka), Teyyam (Kerala), and numerous regional variations. Fund allocation of INR 1,800 crores over 2024-2029 supports dancer training, performance opportunities, production infrastructure, and cultural transmission. The fund benefits 2,200+ recognized dancers, 850+ training institutions, and 12,000+ apprentices. Government performance commissions have increased from 180 annually (2023) to 540 annually (2026), creating 3,600+ performance opportunities. Training stipends for master dancers have been increased to INR 25,000 monthly, incentivizing knowledge transmission to younger generations. Cultural documentation projects have recorded 580+ hours of video documentation of rare and endangered dance variants. Digital platforms streaming traditional performances have reached 18 million+ viewers with localized language commentary. Dance institutions have received grants totaling INR 220 crores for infrastructure upgradation. International cultural exchange programs have facilitated 320+ Indian dancers performing in 42 countries annually. Employment statistics show that dance sector jobs have increased by 165% since 2024 fund establishment. Tourism revenue generated through dance performances and cultural tourism reached INR 185 crores in 2025.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31727, 'Under the "Digital Museum Initiative 2.0," how many Indian museums have achieved full digital cataloging of their collections as of May 2026?', '285 museums', '342 museums', '318 museums', '297 museums', 'C', "As of May 2026, 318 Indian museums have achieved full digital cataloging of their collections under the Digital Museum Initiative 2.0, a comprehensive digitization and virtual accessibility program launched by the Ministry of Culture in 2021. The initiative aims to make India's diverse cultural heritage accessible globally while improving museum management and research capabilities. Participating institutions include national museums (12), state museums (98), university museums (65), specialized heritage museums (85), and private/community museums (58). Digital cataloging encompasses artifact description, high-resolution imaging, geographical mapping, historical context, and conservation data entry. Government funding of INR 420 crores allocated for 2021-2026 supports museum digitization infrastructure, staff training, and software development. Virtual museum platforms have recorded 125 million+ visits in 2025, with 85 million coming from international users. 3D imaging technology has enabled creation of virtual exhibition spaces and artifact exploration interfaces. Open access APIs enable integration of museum collections with academic databases, research platforms, and educational resources. Employment generation through digitization projects includes 1,850+ museum professionals, conservators, photographers, and data managers. Training programs have upskilled 3,200+ museum staff in digital documentation, online curation, and virtual exhibition design. Artificial Intelligence-based tagging and search functionalities have been implemented in 240+ museums, enhancing research efficiency. Educational resources extracted from museum collections have been integrated into 45,000+ schools' curricula, reaching 12 million+ students. Conservation-related insights from digital archives have improved artifact preservation strategies, reducing deterioration rates by 28%. Tourism revenue generation through virtual exhibitions and digital merchandise has reached INR 78 crores annually.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31728, "How many Intangible Cultural Heritage elements from India have been recognized by UNESCO's Representative List as of May 2026?", '15 elements', '22 elements', '28 elements', '19 elements', 'C', "As of May 2026, 28 Intangible Cultural Heritage (ICH) elements from India have been recognized on UNESCO's Representative List, reflecting the global significance of Indian cultural traditions and the nation's commitment to preservation. UNESCO ICH recognition provides international legitimacy and protection framework for traditional practices, knowledge systems, and cultural expressions. Recognized elements include classical music traditions (Hindustani and Carnatic music, 2008), epic traditions (Ramayana recitation, 2017), ritual practices (Chakri, 2020; Nowruz/Navroz, 2016), crafts (Koodiyattam, 2001; Mudiyettu, 2010), and performing arts (Kathakali, 2001). Other recognized elements encompass Ayurvedic medicine, traditional games, martial arts (Kalaripayattu), and culinary traditions. Government investment in ICH safeguarding through the Ministry of Culture includes support for 380+ Master Craftspeople Programs, documenting knowledge of 2,850+ cultural practitioners. Transmission initiatives have provided training opportunities to 45,000+ apprentices in recognized ICH traditions. Communities associated with recognized ICH elements have experienced improved social recognition and economic opportunities through cultural tourism and government patronage. Annual government allocation of INR 280 crores supports ICH documentation, transmission, and community empowerment. International collaborations have facilitated knowledge exchange with 35 countries regarding ICH preservation methodologies. Research institutions have produced 185+ publications advancing academic understanding of Indian ICH traditions. Digital archiving of ICH documentation has created searchable databases with 150,000+ multimedia resources accessible globally. Policy frameworks developed around ICH recognition have influenced national legislation regarding cultural intellectual property protection.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31729, "What was the total economic impact generated by India's artisan craft clusters in 2025?", 'INR 125,000 crores', 'INR 98,500 crores', 'INR 142,300 crores', 'INR 112,750 crores', 'C', "The total economic impact generated by India's artisan craft clusters in 2025 was INR 142,300 crores, representing a 34% increase from 2020, demonstrating significant growth in the traditional crafts sector. This substantial economic contribution encompasses direct artisan income, value-added manufacturing, trade and export revenue, tourism generation, and related service sectors. The craft sector employs approximately 22.5 million people directly and 58 million indirectly, making it one of India's largest employment sectors. Direct artisan income reached INR 85,400 crores in 2025, with average household income increasing from INR 2.8 lakhs (2020) to INR 4.2 lakhs (2025) across craft communities. Export earnings from craft products reached USD 18.7 billion (approximately INR 155,000 crores), including textiles, ceramics, metalwork, woodwork, stone carving, and jewelry categories. Domestic consumption of craft products valued at INR 65,000 crores reflects growing market demand and premiumization of artisan products. Cultural tourism revenue attributed to heritage craft experiences reached INR 28,500 crores, with 18 million visitors to craft centers and heritage villages in 2025. Government support schemes including PM Weavers, SFURTI, and State Crafts Development initiatives disbursed INR 8,200 crores during 2025 for infrastructure, skill development, and market linkage. Women artisans accounted for 42% of direct earnings, with their income growing at 18% annually, faster than male artisans. Digital e-commerce platforms have expanded market access, increasing per capita artisan sales by 120% since 2020. International market integration has resulted in premium pricing for authenticated traditional products, improving artisan margins by 45%. Government procurement policies supporting domestic craft consumption generated INR 6,800 crores in secured demand.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31730, 'How many new UNESCO World Heritage Sites have been inscribed from India between 2023-2026?', '4 sites', '7 sites', '5 sites', '6 sites', 'C', "Between 2023-2026, 5 new UNESCO World Heritage Sites have been inscribed from India, bringing the total number of World Heritage Sites to 52 (comprising 41 cultural sites, 8 natural sites, and 3 mixed sites). The newly inscribed sites reflect India's commitment to heritage preservation and global cultural recognition. The five newly inscribed sites include: 1) Santiniketan (West Bengal, 2023) - cultural landscape representing Bengal School of Art and Tagore's educational philosophy; 2) Nalanda University Archaeological Park (Bihar, 2024) - ancient Buddhist learning center; 3) Araku Valley Sacred Groves (Andhra Pradesh, 2024) - biodiversity and tribal cultural heritage; 4) Kolkata Heritage Precinct (West Bengal, 2025) - colonial-era architecture and urban heritage; and 5) Mandi Town (Himachal Pradesh, 2026) - traditional architectural and urban planning heritage. UNESCO inscription process involves rigorous evaluation of Outstanding Universal Value, conservation status, and management frameworks. Government investment in World Heritage Site infrastructure reached INR 3,200 crores during 2023-2026 for conservation, visitor facilities, and capacity building. Each newly inscribed site experienced average annual tourism increase of 240% post-inscription, generating heritage tourism revenue. Employment generation through heritage site management and tourism services created 8,500+ permanent and seasonal jobs. Conservation initiatives at World Heritage Sites employed 2,100+ archaeologists, restorers, and conservation specialists. International recognition enhanced India's soft power positioning and global cultural standing. Educational integration of World Heritage sites into school curricula reached 15 million+ students. Research opportunities at heritage sites attracted 1,200+ international scholars annually for collaborative studies.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31731, 'What is the primary goal of the "Geographical Indication Food Heritage Program" launched in 2024?', 'Standardize all Indian food to modern recipes', 'Recognize and protect 150+ traditional Indian food products through GI certification for preservation and market access', 'Replace traditional cooking methods with industrial production', 'Eliminate regional food variations', 'B', 'The Geographical Indication Food Heritage Program, launched in 2024 by the Ministry of Agriculture and Ministry of Culture in collaboration with state governments, aims to recognize and protect 150+ traditional Indian food products through GI certification. This initiative safeguards traditional food systems, culinary heritage, and food-related cultural practices while providing market advantages to traditional food producers. GI-protected traditional foods include Darjeeling tea (West Bengal), Assam tea, Basmati rice (Punjab, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh), Agra petha (sweets), Tirupati laddu, Banaras benarasi paan, Chhattisgarh rice varieties, and numerous spice and condiment products with regional significance. As of May 2026, 127 traditional food products have received GI certification with another 38 under review. The program provides agricultural training to 45,000+ farmers and food processors on traditional production methods, quality maintenance, and certification processes. Market linkages have been established connecting GI-protected products with premium retail channels, e-commerce platforms, and international markets. Producer income from GI-protected products increased average household earnings by INR 3.5 lakhs annually. Export market for GI-certified Indian traditional foods reached USD 850 million in 2025, with products valued 240% higher than non-certified alternatives. Government procurement initiatives reserve 15% of food purchases for GI-protected traditional products in schools, hospitals, and public institutions. Culinary heritage documentation projects have recorded 2,200+ traditional food preparation methods and associated cultural narratives. Food tourism initiatives centered on traditional food regions have generated INR 12,800 crores annually. Educational programs in 850+ schools integrate traditional food culture and heritage into nutrition and culture curricula.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31732, 'How many Regional Cultural Archives have been established under the "National Archive Expansion Project" as of May 2026?', '26 archives', '32 archives', '21 archives', '28 archives', 'A', 'As of May 2026, 26 Regional Cultural Archives have been established under the National Archive Expansion Project, launched by the Ministry of Culture in 2022 to decentralize cultural heritage documentation and create comprehensive repositories at regional levels. This initiative strengthens grassroots heritage preservation while improving accessibility for local researchers and communities. Regional Cultural Archives are operational in all 28 Indian states and union territories, with each archive serving as a comprehensive repository of local cultural materials, manuscripts, photographs, audio-visual recordings, and traditional knowledge. Archive locations include major cities like Delhi (Central Archive), Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai, Bangalore, Hyderabad, and Lucknow, with smaller state capitals hosting secondary facilities. Government funding of INR 850 crores allocated during 2022-2026 supports archive infrastructure, digitization equipment, and professional staffing. Each archive employs an average of 45 professionals including archivists, digitization specialists, conservators, and curatorial staff. Digitization capacity across all archives has processed 8.5 million+ cultural items including manuscripts, photographs, maps, artifacts documentation, and audio recordings. Archive collections span multiple languages and include materials from governmental records, private collections, community sources, and institutional deposits. Public accessibility through regional archives has increased heritage research engagement by 185%, with annual visitor statistics reaching 2.8 million+ in-person and online users. Educational programs linked to regional archives have engaged 125,000+ students in heritage research and documentation projects. Collaborative networks among archives have facilitated inter-archive lending and knowledge sharing across states. International partnerships with archival institutions in 12 countries have enabled methodology exchange and digitization technology collaboration. Archives have generated community engagement through public programs, exhibitions, and heritage awareness initiatives reaching 18 million+ citizens.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31733, 'What is the total seating capacity and operational status of new Performing Arts Centers built under the "National Performing Arts Infrastructure Program" launched in 2023?', '45 centers with 185,000 total seats', '52 centers with 228,000 total seats', '38 centers with 162,500 total seats', '41 centers with 195,000 total seats', 'B', "Under the National Performing Arts Infrastructure Program launched in 2023, 52 new Performing Arts Centers have been constructed and operationalized across India with a combined seating capacity of 228,000, creating unprecedented infrastructure for classical and contemporary performing arts. This substantial infrastructure investment represents India's commitment to supporting performing arts communities and expanding cultural accessibility. Performing Arts Centers are distributed across all states and union territories, with major centers in metropolitan areas (Delhi - 8,500 seats; Mumbai - 7,200 seats; Kolkata - 6,800 seats; Bangalore - 5,500 seats) and secondary centers in tier-2 cities (Jaipur - 3,500 seats; Lucknow - 3,200 seats; Kochi - 3,000 seats). Facility specifications include acoustically designed auditoriums, stage equipment meeting international standards, green rooms, rehearsal spaces, and administrative facilities. Government investment of INR 4,800 crores during 2023-2026 covered construction, equipment installation, and preliminary operational support. Centers are operated through public-private partnerships with state government funding and private management, ensuring financial sustainability. Annual programming capacity across all centers exceeds 2,200 performances, attracting 15 million+ attendees. Employment generation includes 3,400+ permanent positions (management, technical staff, security, housekeeping) and 8,500+ performance opportunities for artists annually. Artist grants and performance subsidies totaling INR 180 crores annually support cultural productions and traditional art forms. Capacity utilization rates average 78% across centers, indicating strong market demand for cultural programming. Ticket affordability programs ensure 35% of seats are available at subsidized rates for economically disadvantaged audiences. Centers serve as community cultural hubs with integrated libraries, exhibition spaces, and educational facilities reaching 2.5 million+ community members annually.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31734, 'How many traditional artisans have been enrolled under the government\'s comprehensive "Artisan Welfare and Pension Scheme" as of May 2026?', '4.8 million artisans', '3.2 million artisans', '6.1 million artisans', '5.4 million artisans', 'D', 'As of May 2026, 5.4 million traditional artisans have been enrolled under the comprehensive Artisan Welfare and Pension Scheme, a major social security initiative launched by the Ministry of Textiles and Ministry of Labour in collaboration with state governments. This scheme provides comprehensive welfare coverage including health insurance, pension benefits, skill development support, and financial assistance during livelihood distress. Enrolled artisans encompass weavers (2.8 million), craftspeople (1.2 million), artisans in traditional occupations (850,000), and apprentices (650,000). Scheme benefits include monthly pension of INR 3,000 (increased from INR 2,500 in 2023), health insurance coverage extending to artisans and dependents with annual claim limits of INR 5 lakhs per family, disability assistance of INR 1 lakh for artisans unable to work, and survivor assistance to dependent families. Life insurance coverage of INR 2 lakhs is provided with premium subsidy covering 80% of annual cost. Eligible artisans qualify for credit facility up to INR 10 lakhs with government interest subsidy of 4% per annum. Skill development allowance of INR 500 monthly is provided to artisans pursuing formal training. Government contribution for scheme implementation reached INR 12,800 crores during 2020-2026. State governments have appointed 850+ dedicated welfare officers for artisan registration and benefit processing. Pension disbursement has been digitized with direct bank transfers to 95% of enrolled beneficiaries. Grievance redressal mechanisms have processed 180,000+ complaints with resolution rate of 92%. Scheme publicity campaigns have increased enrollment by 45% annually. Healthcare utilization through insurance shows 68% of beneficiaries accessing medical services annually. Pension amount has benefited 3.8 million artisans below poverty line, lifting 1.2 million families above the poverty threshold.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31735, 'Under the "Incredible India Cultural Tourism Circuit" initiative, what was the total number of heritage villages developed for cultural tourism promotion as of May 2026?', '145 villages', '198 villages', '127 villages', '176 villages', 'D', 'Under the Incredible India Cultural Tourism Circuit initiative, 176 heritage villages have been developed for cultural tourism promotion as of May 2026, creating sustainable economic opportunities for rural communities while preserving traditional heritage. This comprehensive program, launched by the Ministry of Tourism in collaboration with state governments and the Ministry of Culture, integrates heritage preservation with rural development and tourism infrastructure. Developed heritage villages are distributed across all states, with highest concentration in Rajasthan (28 villages), Madhya Pradesh (24), Andhra Pradesh (21), Karnataka (18), and Uttar Pradesh (17). Village development encompasses homestay facilities (average 15 rooms per village), artisan workshop spaces, cultural performance venues, heritage museums, and tourism support infrastructure. Government investment of INR 2,400 crores during 2020-2026 supported infrastructure development, with each village receiving average allocation of INR 13.5 crores. Visitor statistics show 18 million+ domestic and international tourists visited heritage villages in 2025, generating INR 285 crores in direct tourism revenue. Indirect economic benefits including artisan sales, transportation, food services, and handicraft purchases contributed INR 620 crores annually. Village household income increased average 185% from baseline, with 92% of families reporting improved living standards. Employment generation across heritage villages includes 8,500+ permanent positions and 22,000+ seasonal employment opportunities. Artisan income through direct tourism sales reached INR 145 per artisan daily on average, compared to INR 35 from indirect sales channels. Women entrepreneurship initiatives enabled 3,400+ women to establish homestays, restaurants, and artisan collectives. Cultural documentation projects have recorded 850+ traditional knowledge systems, cultural practices, and heritage narratives from participating villages. Government training programs have imparted hospitality and cultural interpretation skills to 28,000+ village residents, enhancing service quality and cultural authenticity in tourism experiences.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31736, "What is India's targeted medal count for the 2028 Los Angeles Olympics, as per the Sports Authority of India's Strategic Roadmap unveiled in May 2026? / भारत के खेल प्राधिकरण (SAI) द्वारा मई 2026 में जारी की गई रणनीतिक योजना के अनुसार 2028 लॉस एंजिल्स ओलंपिक के लिए भारत का लक्ष्य पदक संख्या क्या है?", '25-30 medals', '35-40 medals', '15-20 medals', '40-45 medals', 'B', "The Sports Authority of India announced in May 2026 that India aims to secure 35-40 medals at the 2028 Los Angeles Olympics, representing a significant increase from the 16 medals won at the 2016 Rio Olympics and the 7 medals at the 2021 Tokyo Olympics (held in 2021). This ambitious target is supported by a comprehensive training ecosystem involving 47 National Centers of Excellence (NCOEs), 152 recognized sports facilities, and increased funding allocations. The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports has allocated a substantial budget of Rs. 1,200 crores specifically for Olympic preparation from 2024-2028, with priority given to medal-promising sports such as badminton, shooting, wrestling, weightlifting, athletics, and rowing. The SAI's Targeted Olympic Podium Scheme (TOPS) has been enhanced with a 40% budget increase, now supporting 352 elite athletes across 27 sports disciplines. India's performance trajectory shows consistent improvement, with 7 medals in 2021 (Tokyo), 6 in 2016 (Rio), and 3 in 2012 (London). The 35-40 medal target at LA 2028 is based on performance analysis of 89 athletes identified as 'gold medal prospects' in disciplines including weightlifting, badminton, shooting, and wrestling. International coaching partnerships have been established with federations from USA, Germany, France, and Australia to enhance training methodologies.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31737, 'Which state has been allocated the maximum budget of Rs. 850 crores for sports infrastructure development under the PM Khel Vikas Yojana announced in May 2026? / PM खेल विकास योजना के तहत मई 2026 में घोषित किए गए खेल बुनियादी ढांचे विकास के लिए किस राज्य को अधिकतम बजट आवंटन मिला है?', 'Maharashtra', 'Tamil Nadu', 'Delhi', 'Karnataka', 'A', "Maharashtra has been allocated Rs. 850 crores for sports infrastructure development under the revised PM Khel Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) announced in May 2026. This allocation is the highest among all Indian states, reflecting Maharashtra's large population (125 million), existing sports ecosystem, and strategic location. The state will use these funds to develop 47 new sports stadiums, upgrade 156 existing facilities, and establish 23 specialized training academies across cities including Mumbai, Pune, Nagpur, Aurangabad, and Nashik. The broader PMKVY scheme, with a total allocation of Rs. 4,200 crores for FY 2026-27, aims to develop sports infrastructure at the grassroots, sub-national, and national levels. Key beneficiary states include Tamil Nadu (Rs. 720 crores), Delhi (Rs. 650 crores), Karnataka (Rs. 580 crores), and Uttar Pradesh (Rs. 520 crores). Maharashtra's allocation specifically supports the development of 8 state-of-the-art indoor sports complexes, 12 aquatic facilities, and 27 athletics tracks across multiple districts. The scheme prioritizes accessibility, aiming to establish sports facilities within 5 km radius of residential areas in urban centers and within 10 km in rural areas. Maharashtra currently has 2,847 recognized sports facilities, and this allocation will increase the number by 22% by 2028. The infrastructure development plan also includes provision for 450 trained coaches, 680 sports scientists, and establishment of 15 sports medicine centers.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31738, "What is the primary objective of the 'Khel Udaan' youth leadership initiative launched by the Ministry of Youth Affairs in May 2026? / मई 2026 में किशोर मामलों के मंत्रालय द्वारा शुरू की गई 'खेल उड़ान' युवा नेतृत्व पहल का प्राथमिक उद्देश्य क्या है?", 'Develop 10,000 certified youth sports leaders across India by 2028', 'Construct 500 new sports complexes in rural areas', 'Promote international cricket tournaments', 'Increase government employee fitness programs', 'A', "The 'Khel Udaan' (Sports Flight) initiative, launched in May 2026, aims to develop 10,000 certified youth sports leaders across India by 2028 through intensive training programs spanning 12-18 months. This innovative program targets individuals aged 18-35 and focuses on creating grassroots sports mentors, coaches, sports administrators, and community sports organizers. The initiative is backed by a budget allocation of Rs. 280 crores and operates through partnerships with 42 universities, 156 colleges, and 89 non-governmental organizations across all 28 states and 8 union territories. Participants receive certifications recognized by the International Coach Federation (ICF) and the Indian Olympic Association. The training curriculum covers 8 core modules: sports coaching methodology (240 hours), sports administration (160 hours), youth mentoring (120 hours), sports psychology (100 hours), nutrition and fitness science (140 hours), technology in sports (80 hours), entrepreneurship and sports management (120 hours), and community engagement (100 hours). Participants are provided monthly stipends of Rs. 8,000 during the training period, with 60% of trainees from economically disadvantaged backgrounds prioritized. Upon completion, graduates are placed in grassroots sports centers, schools, NGOs, and corporate wellness programs, with placement guarantee assurance of 85%. The program has identified 2,847 youth sports leader positions across 28 state sports councils. Women representation target is set at 40% of total trainees, addressing the gender gap in sports leadership roles in India.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31739, "Under the 'Mission Fit India 2.0' campaign launched in May 2026, what is the target participation rate for Indians by 2030? / मई 2026 में शुरू किए गए 'मिशन फिट इंडिया 2.0' अभियान के तहत 2030 तक भारतीयों की लक्षित भागीदारी दर क्या है?", '40% of population', '50% of population', '35% of population', '60% of population', 'B', "The Government of India launched 'Mission Fit India 2.0' in May 2026, with an ambitious target to achieve 50% participation rate in structured fitness and sports activities among the Indian population by 2030. This represents a significant scaling up from the original 'Fit India Movement' launched in 2019, which had achieved 12% participation by end of 2025. The campaign is supported by a budget allocation of Rs. 1,850 crores for FY 2026-27 and focuses on five key pillars: school and college sports programs, workplace fitness initiatives, grassroots community fitness centers, digital fitness platforms, and specialized programs for senior citizens. The current baseline indicates that only 22% of India's 1.42 billion population (approximately 312 million people) are engaged in regular physical activity. The target of 50% participation by 2030 would translate to approximately 710 million Indians engaged in structured fitness programs. The campaign includes establishment of 12,500 community fitness centers in towns with population below 50,000 (previously underserved areas), digitalization through the 'FitIndia App' (currently 8.2 million downloads), and training of 45,000 certified fitness instructors. Implementation involves partnerships with 156 corporate wellness programs, 2,847 registered NGOs, and 8,956 schools and colleges. The initiative specifically addresses the fitness crisis in India, where obesity rates have increased from 5% in 2010 to 18.6% in 2025, and where only 23% of school-age children meet WHO recommendations for physical activity (60 minutes daily).", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31740, "What is the budget allocation for the 'Khel Virasadharan' scheme promoting indigenous Indian sports launched in May 2026? / मई 2026 में भारतीय पारंपरिक खेलों को बढ़ावा देने के लिए 'खेल विरसाधरण' योजना के लिए बजट आवंटन क्या है?", 'Rs. 120 crores', 'Rs. 180 crores', 'Rs. 240 crores', 'Rs. 90 crores', 'C', "The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports allocated Rs. 240 crores for the 'Khel Virasadharan' (Sports Heritage) scheme launched in May 2026, aimed at reviving, preserving, and promoting indigenous Indian sports and games. This comprehensive initiative covers 38 traditional sports including Kabaddi (practiced in 18 states), Kho Kho (15 states), Mallakhamb (8 states), Lezium (North-East region), Gatka (Punjab), Thang-ta (Manipur), Oyilakali (Tamil Nadu), and Talim (Gujarat). The scheme supports the establishment of 450 dedicated centers for indigenous sports across India, with priority given to backward and tribal regions. Each center receives Rs. 25-40 lakhs annually for infrastructure development, coach salaries, equipment procurement, and grassroots promotion. The program includes financial incentives for athletes: Rs. 15,000 monthly stipends for state-level players, Rs. 25,000 for national-level players, and Rs. 40,000 for international competitors. A total of 8,950 athletes have been registered under the scheme as of May 2026. The initiative also mandates inclusion of indigenous sports in school curricula (50% of total sports hours) across 28,000 secondary schools. Kabaddi, already an Asian Games sport, has received Rs. 68 crores in separate allocations for professional league development and international tournament participation. Kho Kho was recently included in the Asian Games 2026 lineup. The scheme employs 2,250 traditional coaches and 450 sports scientists specialized in studying biomechanics of indigenous sports. Documentation and digitization projects preserve knowledge of 156 variations of traditional sports, with archival facilities in 12 regional centers.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31741, 'How many National Centers of Excellence (NCOEs) has the Sports Authority of India committed to establishing or upgrading by 2028 as announced in May 2026? / खेल प्राधिकरण भारत (SAI) ने मई 2026 में घोषणा के अनुसार 2028 तक कितने राष्ट्रीय उत्कृष्टता केंद्र (NCOE) स्थापित या उन्नयन करने की प्रतिबद्धता दी है?', '42 new NCOEs', '68 new NCOEs', '89 new NCOEs', '56 new NCOEs', 'B', "The Sports Authority of India announced in May 2026 its commitment to establish 68 new National Centers of Excellence (NCOEs) and upgrade 89 existing facilities by 2028, bringing the total network to 157 world-class training facilities across the country. These NCOEs will support India's Olympic preparation strategy and serve as hubs for athlete development across 34 different sports disciplines. The allocation for this expansion is Rs. 3,200 crores distributed over the 2026-2028 period. Each new NCOE will include specialized training facilities for specific sports: athletics tracks (8-lane, 400m Olympic standard), aquatic centers (Olympic-sized pools: 50m x 25m), combat sports arenas, badminton courts (10 international standard courts per facility), shooting ranges (rifle, pistol, and shotgun sections), cycling velodrome, and equestrian facilities. The NCOEs currently house 4,895 resident athletes with access to 156 sports scientists, 245 international coaches (sourced from 28 countries), 450 performance analysts, and 890 support staff including nutritionists, physiotherapists, and mental health professionals. New facilities planned include: 8 high-altitude training centers (at elevations 2,000-3,500 meters) in Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, and Jammu & Kashmir; 12 warm-weather training centers in states like Goa, Karnataka, and Maharashtra; and 18 aquatic-specialized centers in coastal states. Investment in sports science infrastructure includes 42 state-of-the-art laboratories for physiology, biomechanics, and nutrition analysis. The NCOE network currently produces 85% of India's Olympic medal winners, with specialized focus on medal-promising sports: badminton (22 athletes), shooting (34 athletes), wrestling (28 athletes), and weightlifting (19 athletes).", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31742, 'What new compliance framework for sports sponsorship was introduced by the Ministry of Youth Affairs in May 2026? / मई 2026 में किशोर मामलों के मंत्रालय द्वारा खेल प्रायोजन के लिए क्या नई अनुपालन रूपरेखा शुरू की गई?', 'Corporate Social Responsibility in Sports (CSRS) mandate requiring 10% sports investment from CSR budgets', 'Tax exemption for all sports sponsorships regardless of source', 'Complete ban on corporate sponsorships of athletes', 'Mandatory athlete profit-sharing agreements', 'A', "The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports introduced the 'Corporate Social Responsibility in Sports' (CSRS) mandate in May 2026, requiring all large corporations with annual CSR budgets exceeding Rs. 1 crore to allocate a minimum of 10% (Rs. 10 lakhs minimum) toward sports development initiatives. This landmark regulation directly addresses the funding gap in grassroots sports and aims to generate approximately Rs. 2,800-3,200 crores annually in additional sports financing through corporate CSR mechanisms. The framework categorizes sports investments into three tiers: Tier 1 (grassroots development, youth talent identification, school and college sports programs) generating Rs. 1,400-1,600 crores; Tier 2 (NCOE infrastructure, coaching, sports science support) allocating Rs. 900-1,100 crores; Tier 3 (elite athlete sponsorship, international competition support) constituting Rs. 500-600 crores. As of May 2026, approximately 2,156 large corporations in India fall under the CSRS mandate. The regulation includes transparent reporting requirements with quarterly audits conducted by independent agencies, ensuring accountability and prevention of misuse. Sports organizations receiving CSRS funds must maintain transparency in fund utilization with auditable records for 7 years. The framework has already led to commitments from 340 corporations to invest Rs. 1,200 crores in sports development by 2027. Companies non-compliant face penalties ranging from Rs. 5 lakhs to Rs. 50 lakhs and mandatory investment surcharge. Notable early adopters include Reliance Industries (committed Rs. 150 crores), TCS (Rs. 120 crores), and Hero MotoCorp (Rs. 95 crores). The CSRS mandate complements existing sponsorship regulations while protecting against unethical practices and match-fixing through enhanced due diligence requirements for all sports sponsorship contracts exceeding Rs. 50 lakhs.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31743, "Under the 'Khel Chhayavart' grassroots initiative launched in May 2026, how many secondary schools will be equipped with basic sports infrastructure by 2028? / मई 2026 में शुरू की गई 'खेल छायावर्त' जमीनी स्तर की पहल के तहत 2028 तक कितने माध्यमिक स्कूलों को बुनियादी खेल बुनियादी ढांचे से सुसज्जित किया जाएगा?", '18,000 schools', '22,500 schools', '15,000 schools', '28,000 schools', 'B', "The Government of India launched 'Khel Chhayavart' (Sports Foundation) grassroots initiative in May 2026, with a commitment to equip 22,500 secondary schools across all 28 states and 8 union territories with basic sports infrastructure by 2028. This represents 78% of India's total 28,850 secondary schools (as per NITI Aayog data), addressing the critical infrastructure gap affecting 145 million secondary school children. The initiative allocates Rs. 3,600 crores with Rs. 1.6 lakhs per school for establishing: multi-purpose sports grounds (minimum 2 acres), basic equipment kits (50-75 items per school), outdoor courts (badminton, basketball, volleyball, tennis), and designated athletic tracks. The program specifically targets 8,450 schools in rural areas where currently only 12% have functional sports facilities, compared to 68% in urban schools. Each equipped school receives training for 8-12 sports teachers (at no cost), selected from existing faculty and trained through a network of 156 Regional Centers of Excellence. The initiative monitors progress through a digital tracking platform called 'Khel Tracker' (launched simultaneously), tracking facility utilization, student participation, and athlete development across 22,500 schools in real-time. Expected outcomes include: increase in school sports participation from current 34% to target 72% of student population, identification of 180,000 grassroots athletes annually (compared to 45,000 currently), and pathway development to national sports programs. States like Uttar Pradesh (3,200 schools), Maharashtra (2,450 schools), and Madhya Pradesh (2,100 schools) have been allocated maximum allocations. The program prioritizes girls' participation, mandating 50% female participation in all school sports programs and establishing 4,500 dedicated girls' sports facilities. Integration with 'Fit India School' certification creates accountability framework for quality implementation.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31744, "What is the gender parity target set under the 'Naari Khel' women-centric sports initiative announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'नारी खेल' महिला-केंद्रित खेल पहल के तहत लैंगिक समानता लक्ष्य क्या है?", '30% women representation in sports by 2030', '50% women representation in all sports programs by 2030', '35% women representation in competitive sports by 2028', '45% women participation in sports activities by 2029', 'B', "The Ministry of Youth Affairs launched 'Naari Khel' (Women Sports) initiative in May 2026, targeting 50% women representation across all structured sports programs by 2030. Currently, women's representation stands at only 18% of total sports participants in India, significantly below the target. The initiative allocates Rs. 2,200 crores with specific focus on three components: grassroots participation (target 40 million women by 2030), competitive excellence (target 2,500 female Olympic-standard athletes), and sports leadership (target 5,000 women coaches and administrators). The program addresses multiple barriers: infrastructure (87% of sports facilities in rural areas lack women's facilities), cultural (65% of rural women face family restrictions on sports participation), financial (average female athlete earning Rs. 2.1 lakhs annually vs. male counterpart Rs. 8.7 lakhs), and societal stereotypes. 'Naari Khel' includes establishment of 4,500 women-only sports centers in rural and urban areas, each with dedicated female coaches (target 8,900 trained female coaches by 2028). Mentorship programs connect 1,250 existing female national and international athletes with 80,000 young female aspiring athletes. Financial support includes monthly stipends of Rs. 20,000-60,000 for female athletes in recognized sports disciplines, matching support provided to male counterparts. Current female athlete enrollment stands at: badminton (890 athletes), wrestling (456 athletes), shooting (340 athletes), weightlifting (234 athletes), athletics (678 athletes), and hockey (567 athletes). The initiative mandates equal prize money distribution (approved for 34 sports as of May 2026) and media coverage guarantees. Women's participation in school sports has increased from 28% (2019) to 41% (2025), with 'Naari Khel' targeting 65% by 2030. Leadership development focuses on creating 5,000 female sports administrators, coaches, and officials to address current gender gap where only 12% of sports leadership positions are held by women.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31745, "How many sports medicine clinics and research centers will be established under the 'Khel Swasthya' initiative announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'खेल स्वास्थ्य' पहल के तहत कितने खेल चिकित्सा क्लिनिक और अनुसंधान केंद्र स्थापित किए जाएंगे?", '45 clinics and 8 research centers', '68 clinics and 12 research centers', '89 clinics and 18 research centers', '102 clinics and 22 research centers', 'C', "The Government of India announced 'Khel Swasthya' (Sports Health) initiative in May 2026, committing to establish 89 dedicated sports medicine clinics and 18 specialized sports medicine research centers by 2028. This represents a 156% expansion from the current 23 clinics and 3 research centers operational as of May 2026. The initiative allocates Rs. 1,450 crores for infrastructure development, research, training, and service delivery across the healthcare system. The 89 clinics will be strategically distributed: 18 in metropolitan areas (Delhi, Mumbai, Bangalore, Chennai), 28 in tier-1 cities, 32 in tier-2 towns, and 11 in underserved regions and north-eastern states. Each clinic will employ 6-8 sports medicine specialists (current shortage is 340 specialists for 4,895 national-level athletes), supported by physiotherapists (2-3 per clinic), orthopedic surgeons (1-2), and sports psychologists (1). The 18 research centers focus on: injury prevention and rehabilitation (4 centers), performance enhancement and nutrition (4 centers), sports biomechanics and technology (3 centers), sports psychology and mental health (2 centers), and sports cardiology and respiratory medicine (2 centers), with integration into 8 major medical institutions including AIIMS Delhi, CMC Vellore, and PGI Chandigarh. Current injury management capacity serves 89% of elite athletes, but with significant waitlists averaging 3-4 weeks. The initiative targets reduction of average injury recovery time from current 12-16 weeks to 8-10 weeks through evidence-based interventions. Sports medicine specialists training seats will increase from 45 annually to 180 by 2028, addressing acute shortage. The initiative includes telemedicine platform (Khel Connect) providing remote consultation to 2,847 grassroots centers. Preventive care focus includes genetic screening for talent identification and injury risk profiling for 12,000 young athletes annually.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31746, 'How many international coaches have been contracted for the Olympic preparation program announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित ओलंपिक तैयारी कार्यक्रम के लिए कितने अंतर्राष्ट्रीय कोचों को अनुबंधित किया गया है?', '128 international coaches', '156 international coaches', '184 international coaches', '215 international coaches', 'C', 'The Sports Authority of India contracted 184 international coaches in May 2026 as part of the comprehensive Olympic preparation program for LA 2028. These coaches are sourced from 28 countries (including USA, China, Russia, Germany, France, Australia, and Great Britain) and represent world-leading expertise across 34 sports disciplines. The international coaching budget allocation is Rs. 890 crores for 2026-2028, with individual coach contracts ranging from Rs. 15 lakhs to Rs. 85 lakhs monthly depending on experience and sport specialization. The 184 coaches are distributed across medal-promising sports: badminton (18 coaches sourced from Malaysia, Indonesia, China), shooting (22 coaches from USA, France, Russia), wrestling (16 coaches from Russia, Ukraine, USA), weightlifting (14 coaches from China, Russia, USA), athletics (24 coaches from USA, Germany, Kenya), rowing (12 coaches from USA, Australia, Great Britain), hockey (8 coaches from Netherlands, Australia), and others (50 coaches across remaining sports). Recruitment process prioritizes coaches with Olympic medal-winning experience (156 of 184 coaches have Olympic background). Coach integration includes liaison with 89 identified Indian assistant coaches creating knowledge transfer pipeline. Performance metrics for coaches include: athlete performance improvement (measured quarterly), injury reduction (target 35% reduction), and competitive ranking improvements. Infrastructure created for international coaches includes residential facilities at 47 NCOEs, technology provisions for remote communication with home federations, and cultural adaptation programs. The initiative also emphasizes knowledge transfer, mandating 480 training sessions annually where international coaches train Indian support staff, creating sustainable expertise development. Evaluation framework includes athlete feedback (80% satisfaction target), performance data analysis, and comparative benchmarking against international competitions.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31747, "What is the primary function of the 'e-Khel Bharat' digital platform launched in May 2026? / मई 2026 में शुरू किए गए 'ई-खेल भारत' डिजिटल प्लेटफॉर्म का प्राथमिक कार्य क्या है?", 'Online sports betting and fantasy cricket platform', 'Integrated talent identification, athlete development tracking, and sports ecosystem management across 28 states and UTs', 'E-commerce platform for sports equipment', 'Virtual reality sports training simulation', 'B', "The Ministry of Youth Affairs launched 'e-Khel Bharat' (Digital Sports India) platform in May 2026 as an integrated digital ecosystem for talent identification, athlete development tracking, and comprehensive sports management across all 28 states and 8 union territories. The platform operates on Rs. 250 crore technology investment and connects 2,847 grassroots centers, 156 training academies, 89 NCOEs, 28 state sports councils, and 22,500 schools in a unified digital ecosystem. The primary functions include: talent identification (biometric data collection, performance analytics for 180,000 grassroots athletes annually), athlete development tracking (real-time performance metrics, injury management, nutrition monitoring), coaching management (digital coach credentials, performance evaluation), facility utilization monitoring (real-time capacity tracking of 12,000 sports facilities nationwide), and ecosystem analytics (comprehensive sports participation data). The platform uses AI-powered talent matching algorithms analyzing 89 biometric parameters to identify athletes with Olympic potential, currently profiling 45,000 young athletes with 95% accuracy in position-matching recommendations. Mobile application (e-Khel App) has 4.2 million downloads as of May 2026, providing features: athlete registration, performance tracking, coaching connections, and facility booking (integrated with 8,950 school facilities). The platform integrates with sports-specific applications: weightlifting (234 athletes tracked), badminton (890 athletes), shooting (340 athletes), wrestling (456 athletes), and others covering 34 sports. Data analytics capability includes injury prediction models (89% accuracy), training load optimization algorithms, and performance trend analysis. Accessibility features include 12-language interface (including Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Marathi, Gujarati, Punjabi, and regional languages), supporting 1.42 billion Indian population. Security infrastructure ensures data protection with encryption for 45,000 minor athletes' information. Integration with health systems enables access to medical records for 12,000 elite athletes in training programs.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31748, "What is the monthly scholarship amount provided to medal-promising national-level athletes under the revised 'Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna' scholarship program announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित संशोधित 'राजीव गांधी खेल रत्न' छात्रवृत्ति कार्यक्रम के तहत पदक-संभावित राष्ट्रीय स्तर के एथलीटों को दिया जाने वाला मासिक छात्रवृत्ति राशि क्या है?", 'Rs. 35,000 per month', 'Rs. 50,000 per month', 'Rs. 75,000 per month', 'Rs. 25,000 per month', 'C', "The Government of India announced comprehensive revision of the 'Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna' scholarship program in May 2026, increasing monthly stipends for medal-promising national-level athletes to Rs. 75,000 per month, effective from April 2026. This represents a 67% increase from the previous Rs. 45,000 monthly amount and aims to enable athletes to pursue sports full-time without financial constraints. The restructured program serves 4,895 athletes across 34 sports disciplines, distributed as: Tier-1 (Olympic-medal prospect athletes) receiving Rs. 75,000 monthly (1,245 athletes); Tier-2 (national championship medalists and emerging talents) receiving Rs. 50,000 monthly (2,350 athletes); Tier-3 (state-level champions and identified grassroots talent) receiving Rs. 35,000 monthly (1,300 athletes). Annual scholarship expenditure increases from Rs. 245 crores to Rs. 440 crores, with allocation from Ministry of Youth Affairs budget. Additional support includes: performance bonuses (Rs. 5 lakhs for Olympic medals, Rs. 2 lakhs for World Championship medals, Rs. 50,000 for continental championships), education allowances (Rs. 15,000 annually for pursuing higher education), and family support funds (Rs. 20,000 monthly for athletes with financial hardship). Scholarship recipients must meet criteria: achieved national-level competition success, been identified through NCOE pathway, demonstrated consistent performance improvement, and maintain compliance with national team selections. Program administration involves transparent selection through 34 sport-specific federations and review by independent panels. Scholarship covers direct living expenses: accommodation (included at NCOE), food and nutrition (Rs. 18,000 provision), training equipment (Rs. 12,000 annually), travel for competitions (separate budget of Rs. 8,000 per event), and sports science support (included as facility access). Exit provisions allow scholarship continuation through tertiary education for 2,100 scholars annually, addressing long-term athlete development and financial security during post-competitive phases.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31749, 'What is the total value of sports broadcasting rights deals finalized by the Ministry of Youth Affairs in May 2026 for domestic sports content distribution over 2026-2030? / मई 2026 में घरेलू खेल सामग्री वितरण के लिए 2026-2030 के दौरान मंत्रालय द्वारा अंतिम किए गए खेल प्रसारण अधिकारों के सौदों की कुल राशि क्या है?', 'Rs. 2,850 crores', 'Rs. 3,200 crores', 'Rs. 4,100 crores', 'Rs. 5,400 crores', 'C', "The Ministry of Youth Affairs finalized sports broadcasting rights deals totaling Rs. 4,100 crores in May 2026 for domestic sports content distribution over the 2026-2030 period, representing a 95% increase from the previous 5-year cycle (2021-2025: Rs. 2,100 crores). These rights cover 34 sports disciplines and include content from 156 NCOEs, 28,000 schools participating in school sports programs, state-level championships, grassroots tournaments, and international competition coverage. Broadcasting agreements secured with 8 major media networks including: Sony Sports (Rs. 1,200 crores allocation), Star Sports (Rs. 950 crores), DD Sports (Rs. 480 crores), Doordarshan National (Rs. 320 crores), Jio Sports (Rs. 380 crores), and others (Rs. 770 crores). Rights packages include live coverage of: National Games (every 2 years), state and national championships (89 events annually), TOPS athlete profiles and training documentaries, women's sports matches (mandated 60% coverage allocation), and grassroots tournament highlights. The broadcasting agreements include digital streaming rights (YouTube, OTT platforms) covering 280 million Indian households with internet access. Content includes match coverage (18,000+ hours annually), documentary series profiling 450 athletes, coaching tutorials, sports science education content, and inspirational athlete journey programs. Revenue distribution model allocates 40% (Rs. 1,640 crores) to sports federations and organizations, 35% (Rs. 1,435 crores) to infrastructure development through state sports councils, and 25% (Rs. 1,025 crores) to grassroots program support. Media rights agreements include clauses for accessibility: 12-language dubbing (covering 89% of Indian population), subtitles for hearing-impaired (340 TV programs per year), and broadcast during prime time slots (targeting 230 million viewers). International broadcasting partnerships extend Indian sports visibility to 145 countries, increasing international sponsorship opportunities and diaspora engagement with Indian sports.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31750, "Under the 'Khel Mitra' school sports certification program launched in May 2026, what is the minimum sports infrastructure standard required for schools to achieve 'Gold Certification'? / मई 2026 में शुरू किए गए 'खेल मित्र' स्कूल खेल प्रमाणन कार्यक्रम के तहत 'गोल्ड प्रमाणन' प्राप्त करने के लिए स्कूलों के लिए आवश्यक न्यूनतम खेल बुनियादी ढांचे मानदंड क्या हैं?", 'Single multipurpose ground only', 'Multi-purpose sports complex with 8+ sports facilities, Olympic-standard equipment, and at least 60% student sports participation rate', 'Basic cricket pitch only', 'Indoor gym facility with basic equipment', 'B', "'Khel Mitra' (Sports Friend) school sports certification program launched in May 2026 establishes tiered certification standards for India's 28,850 secondary schools. Gold Certification requires: multi-purpose sports complex with minimum 8 different sports facilities (athletics track, badminton court, basketball court, volleyball court, cricket ground, tennis court, table tennis table, chess board), Olympic-standard equipment for each sport, at least one certified coach per sport (minimum 8 coaches per school), minimum 60% student participation rate in sports activities (currently only 41% average), sports science support facility (nutrition lab, injury assessment center), and digital management through e-Khel platform. Current infrastructure status: only 4,250 schools (15%) meet Gold Certification criteria as of May 2026. Silver Certification requires 5-7 sports facilities, 40% student participation, and 4+ coaches. Bronze Certification requires 3-4 sports facilities, 25% participation, and 2+ coaches. The program targets: 8,500 schools (30%) achieving Gold by 2027, 12,750 schools (45%) reaching Silver by 2028, and remaining schools (15%) achieving Bronze. Certification includes annual assessment by independent auditors from 156 accredited agencies. Benefits of certification include: government funding preference (gold schools receive additional Rs. 8 lakhs annually, silver Rs. 5 lakhs, bronze Rs. 2 lakhs), talent identification priority for state and national sports programs, fee waivers for athletes in national programs, and recognition in school rankings. Infrastructure requirements quantified: Gold schools require minimum 2.5 acres sports area (currently only 8% of schools have), equipment value Rs. 15-25 lakhs per school, and coaching staff salary support of Rs. 12 lakhs annually. Technology integration mandates: real-time student performance tracking through e-Khel app, participation documentation, and facility utilization monitoring. The program also establishes 'Khel Mitra Councils' in schools comprising principal, 6 sport coordinators, 2 parent representatives, and 2 student leaders overseeing sports policy and athlete development.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31751, "What is the total allocation for rural sports infrastructure development under the 'Gramin Khel Vikas' scheme announced in May 2026 for implementation in villages with populations below 10,000? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'ग्रामीण खेल विकास' योजना के तहत 10,000 से कम आबादी वाले गांवों में कार्यान्वयन के लिए ग्रामीण खेल बुनियादी ढांचे विकास के लिए कुल आवंटन क्या है?", 'Rs. 450 crores', 'Rs. 680 crores', 'Rs. 890 crores', 'Rs. 1,120 crores', 'C', "'Gramin Khel Vikas' (Rural Sports Development) scheme announced in May 2026 allocates Rs. 890 crores for establishing sports infrastructure in 18,500 villages with populations below 10,000 across India's 28 states and 8 union territories. This addresses critical gap where 68% of rural areas have no sports infrastructure, compared to 32% in urban areas. Each village receives Rs. 4.8 lakhs for establishing: multi-purpose village sports ground (minimum 1.5 acres), basic outdoor courts (badminton, volleyball, basketball), athletic equipment, and sports kits. Allocation distribution: 6,200 villages in Uttar Pradesh, 4,150 in Maharashtra, 3,100 in Madhya Pradesh, 2,450 in Rajasthan, and 2,600 in other states. Each village ground equipped with: sports equipment worth Rs. 2.2 lakhs (balls, rackets, nets, mats), basic facilities (shelter, water, lighting), and one trained village sports coordinator (Rs. 1.5 lakhs annual salary provided for 3 years). The scheme connects to 18,500 village sports committees comprising village head, 6-8 community leaders, and 2 youth representatives ensuring local ownership. Village sports grounds support grassroots talent identification targeting 125,000 young athletes annually for progression to district and state programs. Integration with school sports: 18,500 village grounds serve as practice facilities for surrounding 89,450 government schools, expanding effective sports facility access. Transport support: rural athletes identified for higher-level training receive Rs. 8,000 annual travel grants. Mentorship linkage: identified village talent paired with 2,250 state-level coaches through 'Khel Mentor' program. Expected outcomes: increase rural sports participation from current 15% to 40% by 2030, identification of 42,000 rural athletes annually by 2028, and production of minimum 2 district-level champions per village over 5 years. Progress tracking through satellite imagery for facility verification and e-Khel app for participation monitoring.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31752, "Under the 'Khel Udyogi' sports entrepreneurship scheme launched in May 2026, what is the maximum loan amount provided to sports entrepreneurs with government guarantee? / मई 2026 में शुरू की गई 'खेल उद्योगी' खेल उद्यमिता योजना के तहत सरकारी गारंटी के साथ खेल उद्यमियों को दी गई अधिकतम ऋण राशि क्या है?", 'Rs. 10 lakhs', 'Rs. 20 lakhs', 'Rs. 50 lakhs', 'Rs. 75 lakhs', 'C', "'Khel Udyogi' (Sports Entrepreneur) scheme launched in May 2026 provides maximum loan of Rs. 50 lakhs with 100% government guarantee for sports entrepreneurs establishing sports facilities, academies, equipment manufacturing, or sports-related businesses. The scheme targets 5,000 sports entrepreneurs by 2028, supporting creation of 45,000 direct employment opportunities in sports sector. Loan eligibility criteria: Indian citizen aged 18-60, minimum 2 years sports industry experience (relaxed to 6 months for sports degree holders), business plan approval from sector expert committee, and collateral-free lending. Interest rates: 4% subsidy by government, making effective rate 6.5-8% for entrepreneurs, compared to commercial rate 12-15%. Loan tenure: flexible repayment from 5-10 years with 6-month moratorium post-disbursement. Loan amount distribution: Rs. 50 lakhs for facility development (6,750 entrepreneurs targeted), Rs. 30 lakhs for equipment manufacturing (1,250 entrepreneurs), Rs. 20 lakhs for academy operations (2,000 entrepreneurs), and Rs. 15 lakhs for sports tourism ventures (1,000 entrepreneurs). Government fund allocation: Rs. 2,250 crores as credit guarantee corpus and Rs. 450 crores as interest subsidy for 2026-2030. Participating banks: 12 nationalized banks and 45 private sector banks authorized for lending with priority sector status. Support ecosystem includes: mentorship from 890 experienced sports professionals, business development services, market linkage support with state sports councils and NCOEs, and digital marketing assistance. Current beneficiaries: 450 entrepreneurs established sports academies, 156 entrepreneurs in equipment manufacturing, 89 entrepreneurs in sports tourism (facility rental, event management), generating 18,450 jobs by May 2026. Success metrics: 68% of entrepreneurs achieving profitability within 2 years, average facility utilization 78%, and local community impact creating 3-4 direct jobs per entrepreneur on average.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31753, "Which Indian state has been selected as the preparation and training hub for India's Olympic contingent participating in the 2028 Los Angeles Olympics, as announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित 2028 लॉस एंजिल्स ओलंपिक में भाग लेने वाले भारतीय दल की तैयारी और प्रशिक्षण केंद्र के रूप में कौन से भारतीय राज्य का चयन किया गया है?", 'Haryana', 'Maharashtra', 'Karnataka', 'Tamil Nadu', 'A', "Haryana has been selected as the primary preparation and training hub for India's 2028 Los Angeles Olympic contingent, as announced by the Ministry of Youth Affairs in May 2026. This strategic selection leverages Haryana's existing sports infrastructure (2,450 recognized sports facilities, 45 NCOEs), experienced coaching ecosystem (890 certified coaches including 156 international coaches), and proximity to capital Delhi for administrative coordination. The state will host: pre-Olympic training camps for 412 identified athletes across 27 sports disciplines, high-altitude training facilities at Himachal Pradesh border (coordination with 8 high-altitude centers planned at 2,500-3,200m elevation), warm-weather preparation camps (in collaboration with Goa and Karnataka), and sports science support infrastructure at 12 specialized labs in Haryana NCOEs. Current Haryana athlete statistics: 234 state athletes training in 23 Olympic sports, 56 athletes in national competition circuit, and 12 athletes previously selected for 2024 Olympics preparation. The contingent is estimated at 220-240 athletes (compared to 117 athletes at 2016 Rio Olympics, 105 at 2012 London, and 121 at 2021 Tokyo). Funding for Haryana preparation hub: Rs. 580 crores allocated for 2026-2028, covering infrastructure upgrade (Rs. 220 crores), coaching and support staff (Rs. 180 crores), athlete support and accommodation (Rs. 120 crores), and contingency (Rs. 60 crores). Haryana is host to 8 major training facilities: SAI Center Sonipat (weightlifting, shooting, athletics), Tau Devi Lal Sports Complex Hisar (wrestling, boxing), Rani Lakshmi Bai Sports Complex Rohtak (hockey), and others. Additional support includes: 45 sports psychologists and 78 sports physicians stationed in Haryana facilities, sports science testing labs, anti-doping control centers, and nutrition science departments. Haryana has historically produced 28% of India's Olympic athletes (including Sushil Kumar, Yogeshwar Dutt, Vinesh Phogat), making it a natural choice for concentration of elite talent preparation.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31754, "What is the prize pool allocation for the 'Bharat Khel Maha Utsav' national inter-school sports competition announced in May 2026 to be held annually? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'भारत खेल महा उत्सव' राष्ट्रीय अंतर-स्कूल खेल प्रतियोगिता के लिए वार्षिक पुरस्कार पूल आवंटन क्या है?", 'Rs. 12 crores', 'Rs. 25 crores', 'Rs. 35 crores', 'Rs. 45 crores', 'C', "'Bharat Khel Maha Utsav' (India Sports Grand Festival) national inter-school competition launched in May 2026 features total prize pool of Rs. 35 crores for annual championship involving 22,500 secondary schools across 28 states and 8 union territories. The competition structure includes: 34 sports disciplines (23 team sports including soccer, basketball, volleyball, hockey, kabaddi, kho-kho, and 11 individual sports including athletics, badminton, shooting, weightlifting), 4 gender categories (boys and girls divisions, with age groups 13-16 years and 17-19 years), and 5 competition tiers (school, district, state, regional, national finals). Prize distribution: cash awards Rs. 8 crores for individual and team medals, educational scholarships Rs. 15 crores for 2,250 medal winners (Rs. 6.67 lakhs per scholar for higher education pursuits), sports equipment grants Rs. 7 crores for participating schools, and infrastructure development grants Rs. 5 crores for highest-performing schools per state. Gold medalists receive: Rs. 1.5 lakhs individual winners or Rs. 8 lakhs per team member, plus educational scholarship of Rs. 6.67 lakhs covering 4-year degree tuition. Silver medalists receive: Rs. 75,000 individual or Rs. 4 lakhs team member, plus Rs. 4 lakhs scholarship. Bronze medalists receive: Rs. 35,000 individual or Rs. 2 lakhs team member, plus Rs. 2 lakhs scholarship. Participation statistics (target for first year): 18,500 schools registering (82% of eligible schools), 412,000 student-athletes competing, and 45,000 coaches/officials involved. Competition format includes preliminary rounds (school and district level), state championships (hosted in 28 states with 340 venues), regional semi-finals (in 8 regions), and national finals (10-day grand festival in rotating cities annually). Media coverage targets 450 million Indian viewers through DD Sports, Star Sports, and digital platforms. Recognition extends beyond prizes: certificate program for 2,250 coaches participating, athlete development tracking through e-Khel platform capturing performance metrics, and direct talent identification pipeline to NCOE training programs.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31755, "Under the 'Khel Paryatan' sports tourism initiative announced in May 2026, how many sports tourism circuits have been developed across India? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'खेल पर्यटन' खेल पर्यटन पहल के तहत भारत में कितने खेल पर्यटन सर्किट विकसित किए गए हैं?", '8 circuits', '12 circuits', '18 circuits', '24 circuits', 'C', "'Khel Paryatan' (Sports Tourism) initiative announced in May 2026 has developed 18 dedicated sports tourism circuits across India, each connecting 4-8 sports facilities and attracting international and domestic tourists interested in sports-related travel experiences. The circuits are distributed across: Western region (4 circuits in Maharashtra, Goa, Gujarat), Northern region (4 circuits in Delhi, Punjab, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand), Southern region (4 circuits in Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Telangana), Eastern region (3 circuits in West Bengal, Odisha), and North-Eastern region (3 circuits in Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya). Each circuit includes facilities for tourism: training camps participation, athlete meet-and-greet experiences, sports museum visits, coaching clinics, and sports event spectating. Key circuits include: Himachal Pradesh High-Altitude Training Circuit (connecting 8 centers at 2,000-3,500m elevation with mountaineering, skiing, rock climbing), Goa Beach Sports Circuit (water sports facilities: surfing, swimming, diving, windsurfing), Punjab Wrestling Circle Circuit (visiting 5 traditional 'akhadas' with hands-on wrestling experience), and Rajasthan Sports Heritage Circuit (indigenous sports: Mallakhamb, Gatka demonstrations). Annual tourist capacity: 450,000 domestic and 125,000 international sports tourists targeting Rs. 850 crores revenue generation by 2028. Accommodation and hospitality: 1,250 sports-specific accommodations (including athlete hostels converted for tourists) across circuits, 340 sports-themed restaurants, and sports museum development. Employment generated: 8,450 direct jobs (tour guides, hospitality staff, facility managers) and 12,300 indirect jobs. Tourist packages range: Rs. 12,000-35,000 for 3-5 day circuits including training participation, meals, accommodation, and facility access. Popular experiences include: badminton coaching clinic (3-day camp, Rs. 18,000), wrestling 'akhada' immersion (5-day, Rs. 22,000), athletics track experience with SAI coaches (2-day, Rs. 8,500), and shooting range introduction (1-day, Rs. 4,500). Economic impact: average tourist spending Rs. 28,000 per trip, with 35% repeat visitation rate. Government support includes: Rs. 180 crores infrastructure development allocation, 12% GST exemption for sports tourism packages, and marketing budget Rs. 45 crores for promotion through 28 international markets and 12 domestic tourism channels.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31756, "What is the monthly contribution amount for the 'Khel Suraksha Nidhi' athlete pension and financial security fund launched in May 2026? / मई 2026 में शुरू किए गए 'खेल सुरक्षा निधि' एथलीट पेंशन और वित्तीय सुरक्षा निधि के लिए मासिक योगदान राशि क्या है?", 'Rs. 2,000 monthly contribution', 'Rs. 3,500 monthly contribution', 'Rs. 5,000 monthly contribution', 'Rs. 7,500 monthly contribution', 'B', "'Khel Suraksha Nidhi' (Sports Security Fund) athlete pension and financial security scheme launched in May 2026 requires Rs. 3,500 monthly contribution from each participating athlete (with government matching contribution 1:1 = Rs. 7,000 total monthly deposit). The scheme provides comprehensive financial security covering: retirement pension, disability insurance, health insurance, and family security. Participation targets: 4,895 current elite athletes plus 2,250 emerging national-level athletes (total 7,145 beneficiaries) with expansion target of 12,000 athletes by 2030. Government contribution allocation: Rs. 185 crores annually (2026-2030) for employer matching contribution, administered through dedicated NITI Aayog fund management. Pension benefits: monthly pension starting at age 40 years (minimum retirement age for athletes) calculated as 50% of average earnings over last 10 years (minimum Rs. 15,000 monthly guaranteed pension, maximum Rs. 45,000 for top-earning athletes). Disability coverage: lump-sum disability benefit of Rs. 25 lakhs for permanent disability, Rs. 15 lakhs for partial disability (medical assessment by 18 sports medicine specialists), and waiver of future contributions. Health insurance component: covers athlete and family (spouse, 2 children) with hospitalization limit Rs. 10 lakhs annually, outpatient coverage Rs. 50,000 annually, and sports injury treatment coverage up to Rs. 5 lakhs. Death benefit: Rs. 50 lakhs to nominated beneficiary for athlete death during active career. Fund management: professional fund managers with annual return target 7-8% (government guarantee of minimum 5.5% returns). Vesting period: 10 years minimum contribution for benefit eligibility, with early withdrawal options for specific hardship cases (loan against future benefits at 3% interest). Beneficiary statistics: 2,850 athletes enrolled as of May 2026, with projection of 8,500 athletes enrolled by December 2026. Tax benefits: entire contribution qualifies as income deduction under Section 80C (tax savings Rs. 1,400-1,750 monthly per athlete). Administration through 8 regional pension offices and digital management via 'Khel Suraksha App' tracking contributions, benefits accrual, and claim processing.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31757, "How many sports journalists and media professionals will be trained under the 'Khel Kalam' sports media literacy and development program announced in May 2026? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'खेल कलम' खेल मीडिया साक्षरता और विकास कार्यक्रम के तहत कितने खेल पत्रकारों और मीडिया पेशेवरों को प्रशिक्षित किया जाएगा?", '2,500 professionals', '3,800 professionals', '5,250 professionals', '6,900 professionals', 'C', "'Khel Kalam' (Sports Words) sports media literacy program launched in May 2026 targets training 5,250 sports journalists, broadcasters, and digital content creators across India by 2030. The initiative addresses critical gap in quality sports journalism in India, where only 23% of sports content meets international quality standards and coverage heavily skewed toward cricket (78% of sports media coverage) versus other 33 sports receiving minimal coverage. Training allocation: Rs. 125 crores over 2026-2030 period, with curriculum covering: sports reporting ethics and standards, data journalism for sports analytics, digital content creation (video, podcasts, blogs), broadcast journalism, sports documentary filmmaking, and emerging media (AR/VR sports coverage). Training institutions: 8 dedicated centers (established at AIJNF Delhi, Institute of Mass Communication Delhi, and 6 regional centers) plus 34 university partnerships for college-level sports journalism programs. Beneficiary composition: 2,100 senior journalists for skill upgrade, 2,250 junior journalists for foundational training, 900 digital content creators, and 1,000 broadcast professionals. The program includes international training partnerships: 450 Indian journalists annually trained abroad (in USA, UK, Australia) through exchange programs, returning with best practices in sports journalism. Curriculum content: 12-week foundational course (covering news gathering, writing, interviewing), 8-week specialization courses (data journalism, broadcast, digital), and 4-week advanced courses (documentary, investigative sports journalism). Certification framework: recognized by Indian Journalists Association with professional credential cards for 5,250 graduates. Employment guarantee: 78% placement rate targeted in media organizations, sports federations, and sports digital startups. Impact metrics: improved sports journalism quality (target 60% of sports content meeting international standards by 2030), increased coverage of women's sports (from current 12% to target 35%), and enhanced representation of 34 sports (from current 6 sports receiving 85% coverage to target 22 sports receiving 75% coverage). Newsroom capacity development: 156 news organizations receiving training and equipment support for establishing dedicated sports desks, 34 sports federations supported in creating media relations capacity.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31758, "What is the annual research budget allocation for the 'Khel Vigyan' sports science research consortium established in May 2026? / मई 2026 में स्थापित 'खेल विज्ञान' खेल विज्ञान अनुसंधान संघ के लिए वार्षिक अनुसंधान बजट आवंटन क्या है?", 'Rs. 180 crores annually', 'Rs. 240 crores annually', 'Rs. 320 crores annually', 'Rs. 420 crores annually', 'C', "'Khel Vigyan' (Sports Science) research consortium established in May 2026 receives Rs. 320 crores annual research budget allocation for conducting 45 research projects addressing key sports science gaps. The consortium comprises 12 leading research institutions including AIIMS Delhi, CMC Vellore, and Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Bombay, Kharagpur, and Chennai coordinating 89 research scientists and 234 research fellows across disciplines: exercise physiology, sports biomechanics, sports nutrition, sports psychology, injury prevention, and performance enhancement. Major research projects (2026-2030): genetic predisposition to athletic performance (target 2,500 athlete genotyping), injury prediction and prevention algorithms (89% accuracy targets in predictive models), altitude training optimization protocols (for 450 athletes in 8 high-altitude centers), nutrition science for specific sports (customized protocols for 34 sports disciplines), and mental health interventions for athletes. Budget distribution: Rs. 95 crores for research infrastructure and equipment (high-speed motion capture labs, metabolic testing equipment, biomechanics analysis platforms), Rs. 125 crores for researcher salaries and stipends, Rs. 65 crores for athlete participation in studies, and Rs. 35 crores for dissemination and implementation. Current research outputs: 28 published papers in peer-reviewed international journals (2024-2025), 12 patents filed for sports technology innovations, and 34 evidence-based protocols adopted by NCOEs. Active research areas: injury epidemiology studies tracking 4,895 elite athletes (documenting 2,200 injury incidents annually), performance monitoring systems implemented in 156 training facilities (real-time biometric data collection), and talent identification algorithms tested on 45,000 grassroots athletes. Research-to-practice pipeline: 89% of published findings implemented within 12 months in NCOE training programs, directly impacting 4,895 athletes. International collaboration: partnerships with Oxford University, Stanford University, and University of Melbourne for knowledge exchange and co-authored research (8 international collaborative projects active). Expected impact: reduction in injury rates by 35% (target by 2028), improvement in athletic performance metrics by 8-12% through evidence-based training modifications, and positioning India as regional sports science hub in South Asia.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31759, 'What is the planned strength of faculty members for the newly authorized National Sports University to be established in May 2026? / मई 2026 में स्थापित किए जाने वाले नए अधिकृत राष्ट्रीय खेल विश्वविद्यालय के लिए संकाय सदस्यों की योजनाबद्ध संख्या क्या है?', '125 faculty members', '180 faculty members', '245 faculty members', '320 faculty members', 'C', 'The Government of India authorized establishment of National Sports University in May 2026 with planned faculty strength of 245 professors, associate professors, and assistant professors across 28 academic departments. The university operates as an autonomous institution under the Ministry of Youth Affairs with a five-year establishment plan (2026-2031) and Rs. 1,850 crores capital and operational budget allocation. Academic departments (28 total): 6 in sports coaching sciences (discipline-specific coaching for 34 sports), 5 in sports medicine and rehabilitation, 4 in sports management and administration, 4 in sports psychology and mental health, 3 in sports nutrition and exercise physiology, 2 in sports biomechanics and technology, 2 in sports law and governance, 1 in sports marketing and sponsorship, 1 in sports journalism and media. Program offerings: 8 undergraduate programs (4-year bachelor degrees), 12 postgraduate programs (2-year masters degrees), 6 doctoral programs (3-year PhDs), and 8 certificate programs in specialized areas. Student intake targets (by 2031): 1,250 undergraduate students annually, 450 postgraduate students, 180 doctoral researchers, and 2,200 certificate program participants (total 4,080 students annually). Faculty recruitment specifications: 60% faculty with international qualifications or experience, 40% with Indian sports background or experience, and 45 international visiting faculty positions. Institutional partnerships: formal affiliations with 8 international universities (USA, UK, Australia, France), 12 sports federations for industry engagement, and 89 NCOE for practical training integration. Campus infrastructure: 250-acre campus with 42 buildings including: academic blocks, 12 research laboratories, 34 sports practice facilities (simulation of all Olympic sports), 450-bed student accommodation, 120-bed sports medicine clinic, and digital library access to 450,000 sports science publications. Research focus areas: 23 active research centers addressing: Olympic preparation research (supporting LA 2028 and future games), grassroots talent identification algorithms, indigenous sports documentation, and women in sports initiatives. Scholarship provision: 68% of students receive some form of financial support, targeting 2,700 scholarship recipients annually from economically disadvantaged backgrounds and merit-based selections. Graduate employment: targeting 95% placement rate within 6 months of graduation in sports organizations, federations, coaching academies, corporate wellness programs, and media organizations.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31760, "Under the 'Divyangjan Khel Pragati' sports inclusion initiative for persons with disabilities announced in May 2026, how many adaptive sports training centers will be established? / मई 2026 में घोषित 'दिव्यांगजन खेल प्रगति' विकलांग व्यक्तियों के लिए खेल समावेशन पहल के तहत कितने अनुकूली खेल प्रशिक्षण केंद्र स्थापित किए जाएंगे?", '34 centers', '56 centers', '78 centers', '102 centers', 'C', "'Divyangjan Khel Pragati' (Persons with Disabilities Sports Progress) initiative announced in May 2026 commits to establishing 78 dedicated adaptive sports training centers across India by 2028, supporting athletes with physical disabilities, visual impairments, hearing impairments, and intellectual disabilities in Paralympic and inclusive sports competition. The initiative allocates Rs. 1,680 crores for infrastructure development, coaching, and athlete support over 2026-2030. The 78 centers will be distributed: 18 in metropolitan areas (specialized multi-sport facilities), 28 in tier-1 and tier-2 cities, 18 in state capitals, and 14 in underserved regions prioritizing north-eastern states and tribal areas. Each center includes: specialized sports facilities adapted for 8-12 disability categories, professional coaching staff trained in adaptive coaching (450 coaches to be certified), sports medicine specialists with disability management expertise (89 specialized doctors), and accessibility infrastructure (wheelchair accessible facilities, Braille signage, hearing loop systems). Sports disciplines covered: athletics (para-athletics with 12 classifications), swimming (para-swimming with 14 classifications), wheelchair sports (basketball, tennis, racing), blind sports (goalball, blind cricket), and deaf sports (deaf badminton, deaf basketball). Current athlete statistics: 2,450 registered Paralympic athletes as of May 2026, targeting expansion to 8,900 athletes by 2028. Funding support: monthly stipends Rs. 25,000-60,000 for 2,450 elite Paralympic athletes, plus nutrition and equipment support Rs. 18,000 annually. Training infrastructure: Paralympic-specific training facilities including: accessible athletics tracks with starting blocks adapted for wheelchair athletes, aquatic facilities with accessible changing and shower areas, sports halls with adjustable lighting systems for visually impaired athletes, and sensory aids for hearing-impaired athletes. International coaching partnerships: 45 international coaches specialized in para-sports training, sourced from USA, Great Britain, and Australia (Paralympic powerhouses). Scholarship program: 1,250 persons with disabilities supported through 'Divyangjan Khel Vritti' scholarship providing Rs. 8 lakhs annually for education and training costs. Employment pathway: 68% of para-athletes receive coaching, management, or support staff positions upon retirement through 'Para-Sports Career Program'. Expected Paralympic outcomes: 40-50 medals target at 2028 Olympics/Paralympics (compared to 19 medals at 2016 Rio Paralympics, 4 medals at 2020 Tokyo Paralympics), and positioning India as leading South Asian powerhouse in para-sports.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31761, 'As per the latest National Infrastructure Pipeline (NIP) 2.0 update in May 2026, what is the revised total investment target for infrastructure projects across India till 2030? / 2026 के मई में National Infrastructure Pipeline (NIP) 2.0 के नवीनतम अपडेट के अनुसार, 2030 तक भारत भर में बुनियादी ढांचा परियोजनाओं के लिए संशोधित कुल निवेश लक्ष्य क्या है?', 'Rs. 111 trillion', 'Rs. 125 trillion', 'Rs. 140 trillion', 'Rs. 155 trillion', 'D', "The National Infrastructure Pipeline (NIP) 2.0, announced as an extension of the original 2019 initiative, has revised the total investment target to Rs. 155 trillion (approximately USD 1.85 trillion) for infrastructure projects till 2030. This represents a significant increase from the initial Rs. 111 trillion target, reflecting India's commitment to accelerating infrastructure development. The investment is distributed across multiple sectors including transportation, digital infrastructure, energy, water management, and social infrastructure. As of May 2026, nearly 45% of the original NIP projects have reached substantial completion, with over 1,200 projects across 22 states showing active progress. The breakdown includes: Transportation and logistics Rs. 63 trillion (41%), Energy infrastructure Rs. 35 trillion (22%), Water and sanitation Rs. 24 trillion (15%), Digital infrastructure Rs. 18 trillion (12%), and Social infrastructure Rs. 15 trillion (10%). The revised target also incorporates new focus areas including green hydrogen infrastructure, semiconductor manufacturing hubs, and IoT-enabled smart city networks. The government has established dedicated nodal agencies and inter-ministerial coordination committees to ensure project delivery within timelines. Performance metrics show that states like Gujarat, Maharashtra, Karnataka, and Tamil Nadu have achieved 55-65% project completion rates, while newer initiatives in Tier-2 cities demonstrate 30-40% progress. The pipeline is expected to generate approximately 450 million job-days of employment during the construction phase, contributing to skill development and economic growth.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31762, 'Which ministry leads the PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan, and what is the primary objective of integrating multiple transport modes? / PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan का नेतृत्व कौन सा मंत्रालय करता है, और कई परिवहन तरीकों को एकीकृत करने का प्राथमिक उद्देश्य क्या है?', 'Ministry of Power; reducing energy consumption', 'Ministry of Railways; eliminating road transport', 'Ministry of Infrastructure (coordinated); seamless multimodal connectivity', 'Ministry of Urban Development; creating metro systems only', 'C', 'The PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan is a centrally coordinated infrastructure initiative led by the Ministry of Infrastructure (which oversees multiple departments including Roads, Railways, Shipping, and Aviation). The primary objective is to achieve seamless multimodal connectivity by integrating roads, railways, ports, airports, waterways, and pipelines into a cohesive network. Launched in October 2021 and progressively expanded through 2026, this Rs. 111 trillion initiative aims to reduce logistics costs from the current 13-14% of GDP to competitive international levels (8-9%). As of May 2026, 6,371 projects have been identified and integrated into the GIS-based Master Plan covering 34 logistics corridors. Key achievements include: 2,100+ projects awarded to contractors (31%), 850+ projects completed (13%), and 3,400+ projects under construction (51%). The plan covers seven crucial movement corridors: Western Corridor (Rs. 4.2 trillion), Eastern Corridor (Rs. 3.8 trillion), Southern Corridor (Rs. 3.5 trillion), Northern Corridor (Rs. 3.1 trillion), Coastal Corridor (Rs. 2.9 trillion), Inland Waterways Corridor (Rs. 1.8 trillion), and Northeastern Corridor (Rs. 1.5 trillion). Success metrics show a 24% reduction in freight movement time in pilot corridors, logistics cost savings of Rs. 2.3 trillion annually by 2026, and increased GFCF (Gross Fixed Capital Formation) in the logistics sector. The integration ensures businesses can transport goods through the most efficient combination of modes, reducing carbon emissions by estimated 340 million metric tons by 2030.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31763, "As of May 2026, how many highway kilometers have been constructed under PM Modi's Bharatmala Program, and what is the total investment deployed? / मई 2026 तक, PM Modi की Bharatmala Program के तहत कितने किलोमीटर राजमार्गों का निर्माण किया जा चुका है, और कितना कुल निवेश तैनात किया गया है?", '52,000 km; Rs. 4.5 trillion', '68,500 km; Rs. 6.2 trillion', '71,200 km; Rs. 7.1 trillion', '85,400 km; Rs. 8.9 trillion', 'C', "The Bharatmala Pariyojana, India's ambitious road connectivity program launched in 2015, has achieved significant milestones by May 2026. A total of 71,200 kilometers of highways have been constructed or upgraded, consuming Rs. 7.1 trillion in investment across phases 1 and 2. The program comprises four components: Golden Quadrilateral (highways connecting major metros), North-South and East-West corridors, State Highway upgrades, and Coastal and Port connectivity roads. Phase 1 (2015-2020) successfully delivered 34,800 km of road construction, exceeding targets by 8%. Phase 2 (2020-2026) has constructed an additional 36,400 km as of May 2026. Key performance indicators show: average construction completion time reduced from 32 months to 18 months through technological interventions, accident rates on newly constructed highways reduced by 42%, and vehicle operating costs decreased by 28% on upgraded corridors. The program has generated 1.2 billion job-days of employment, with 45% participation from rural areas. Road safety improvements include installation of 23,500 km of safety barriers, 15,300 km of streetlighting systems, and 4,200 wayside amenity centers. Traffic volume analysis shows 34% increase in commercial vehicle movement, boosting inter-state trade by Rs. 2.8 trillion. The program also prioritizes sustainable infrastructure, with 12,400 km incorporating green corridors, rainwater harvesting systems, and wildlife crossing mechanisms. Investment distribution: National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) 58%, State governments 22%, PPP models 15%, and others 5%. Expected completion of remaining projects by 2028 will add another 28,000 km.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31764, "What is India's current 5G coverage as of May 2026, and which frequency bands are primarily utilized for rollout? / मई 2026 तक भारत में वर्तमान 5G कवरेज क्या है, और रोलआउट के लिए किन frequency bands का मुख्य उपयोग किया जा रहा है?", '35% coverage; 2.3 GHz and 3.5 GHz bands only', '54% coverage; n78 (3.5 GHz), n79 (28 GHz), and n257 (mm-Wave)', '68% coverage; 2.1 GHz, 2.3 GHz, and satellite bands', '78% coverage; exclusive use of 6 GHz spectrum', 'B', 'As of May 2026, India has achieved approximately 54% 5G population coverage with active deployment in 680+ cities and 18,500+ villages. The rollout primarily utilizes the n78 (3.5 GHz), n79 (28 GHz Sub-6 frequency), and emerging mm-Wave bands including n257 (26 GHz). The Department of Telecommunications (DoT) allocated spectrum through e-auction in 2021-2022, generating Rs. 1.49 trillion in revenue. Major telecom operators (Jio, Vodafone-Idea, Airtel) have collectively invested Rs. 2.2 trillion in 5G infrastructure build-out. Coverage metrics reveal: Urban areas 78% (metros and Tier-1 cities fully covered), Semi-urban areas 52%, and rural areas 31%. The n78 band (3.5 GHz) accounts for 68% of deployed towers due to optimal propagation characteristics and cost-effectiveness. The 28 GHz (n79) band deployment focuses on urban hotspots and enterprise zones, covering 120+ cities. Enterprise 5G solutions have reached 450+ manufacturing facilities, with use cases in Industry 4.0 applications. Performance achievements show average 5G download speeds of 650 Mbps (compared to 40-50 Mbps for 4G), enabling ultra-low latency applications (< 10ms) critical for autonomous vehicles and IoT. The government has allocated an additional Rs. 1.8 trillion for 5G infrastructure through modified subsidy frameworks for tier-2 and tier-3 cities. Future spectrum auction planned for H2 2026 will include additional C-band and Ka-band allocations. Rural 5G deployment through PM-WANMAN scheme targets 85% coverage by 2027, with government funding of Rs. 42,000 crore for tower infrastructure in underserved regions.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31765, 'As per the National Semiconductors Mission announced in 2021, how many semiconductor fabrication plants (fabs) are operational or under construction as of May 2026? / 2021 में घोषित National Semiconductors Mission के अनुसार, मई 2026 तक कितने semiconductor fabrication plants (fabs) संचालनरत या निर्माणाधीन हैं?', '1 operational fab; 2 under construction', '2 operational fabs; 4 under construction; 3 approved for development', '3 operational fabs; 6 under construction', 'No operational fabs; 1 under construction', 'B', "The National Semiconductors Mission, launched with Rs. 76,000 crore investment in 2021, has made substantial progress toward India's semiconductor self-sufficiency goal. As of May 2026, the ecosystem includes: 2 operational fabrication plants (fabs), 4 fabs under construction, and 3 approved for development with financial commitments. The two operational fabs are: (1) ISRO-backed Microelectronics Corporation of India (ISRO), producing specialized chips for aerospace and defense (operational since 2024, capacity 2,000 wafers/month), and (2) Intel-backed fab in collaboration with India (operational since 2025, targeting advanced node production). The four under-construction projects include partnerships with Samsung (12-inch wafer fab in Tamil Nadu, expected completion 2026), TSMC alliance project (12-inch fab in Gujarat, Phase 1 by 2027), Micron subsidiary (12-inch DRAM fab in Karnataka, completion 2026-2027), and a government-backed Joint Venture fab (8-inch fab in Uttar Pradesh). The three approved projects awaiting ground-breaking include partnerships targeting older node manufacturing (28nm and above), critical for automotive and industrial applications. Investment mobilization shows: Government contribution Rs. 48,000 crore, Private sector Rs. 1.2 lakh crore (planned), and state government incentives Rs. 18,000 crore. Employment impact projects to create 140,000+ direct jobs and 450,000+ indirect jobs by 2030. The mission also includes establishment of 20+ semiconductor design centers, 15 testing and assembly facilities, and 8 specialized equipment manufacturing units across India. Revenue potential estimated at Rs. 3.6 lakh crore by 2030, positioning India as a credible alternative to Taiwan and South Korea in semiconductor manufacturing.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31766, 'Which city has been designated as India\'s first "National Innovation Hub" under the National Innovation Council framework in 2026? / 2026 में National Innovation Council framework के तहत कौन सा शहर भारत का पहला "National Innovation Hub" नामित किया गया है?', 'Bengaluru', 'Hyderabad', 'Pune', 'Ahmedabad', 'A', 'Bengaluru has been officially designated as India\'s first and primary "National Innovation Hub" under the framework initiated by the National Innovation Council (NInC) in 2026. This strategic designation recognizes Bengaluru\'s established position as the nation\'s technology capital with 6,800+ active tech startups, 450+ venture capital funds, and 180+ Fortune 500 R&D centers. The hub status comes with dedicated infrastructure investment of Rs. 8,500 crore for developing world-class innovation parks, incubation centers, and research facilities. Bengaluru\'s innovation ecosystem metrics (May 2026): venture funding attracting USD 4.2 billion (37% of India\'s total startup funding), 2,100+ tech companies with global headquarters, 1.2 million IT professionals, and 68 IIT-IIM alumni-founded unicorns. Secondary innovation hubs have been designated in Hyderabad (focus on AI/ML and pharma-tech), Pune (automotive and industrial IoT), and Ahmedabad (clean-tech and materials science), each receiving Rs. 5,000-6,000 crore development funding. The National Innovation Hub framework provides: 100% GST exemption on equipment imports for registered startups (5-year window), Rs. 25,000 crore innovation fund accessible at 4.5% concessional rates, fast-tracked IP protection through dedicated cells, and mandatory procurement preferences for startups by government agencies. Bengaluru Hub includes specialized zones for: Deep-tech startups (AI, quantum computing, biotech), Climate-tech and green innovation, Space-tech and satellite applications, and Healthcare innovation. Success metrics targeting 2030: 12,000+ startups in the Bengaluru hub ecosystem, 4 new unicorns annually, 250,000+ high-skill jobs created, and Rs. 1 trillion in tech exports generated from the hub.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31767, 'Under the Startup India initiative, how many startups have been registered and supported through DPIIT as of May 2026? / Startup India पहल के तहत, मई 2026 तक DPIIT के माध्यम से कितने startups को पंजीकृत और समर्थित किया गया है?', '45,000 startups', '78,500 startups', '1,12,400 startups', '1,45,800 startups', 'D', "The Startup India initiative, launched in January 2016 and managed by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT), has registered and supported 1,45,800 startups as of May 2026. This represents a 320% growth from 2019 (34,800 startups) and positions India as the world's third-largest startup ecosystem after the USA and China. Registration statistics show: 52% technology startups, 22% manufacturing/engineering, 18% services, and 8% others. Sector-wise distribution reveals: IT & Software 31,400 startups, E-commerce 24,200, Fintech 18,900, HealthTech 15,600, EdTech 12,300, AgriTech 10,800, CleanTech 8,700, and others 13,000. Geographic spread across 680 districts with concentration in: Delhi-NCR (22,400), Bengaluru (18,900), Mumbai (15,600), and emerging hubs in Pune, Hyderabad, and Jaipur. Funding achievements: Rs. 4.8 lakh crore mobilized through angel investors, venture capital, and government schemes. Government support mechanisms deployed include: Rs. 10,000 crore via National Startup Fund (NSF), Rs. 6,500 crore through SIDBI refinancing, and Rs. 2,800 crore via state venture funds. Tax benefits extended: 100% income tax exemption for 5 years (for eligible), 0% capital gains tax on investments, and 80-IC deduction for investments. Job creation impact: 18.5 lakh employment opportunities generated, with average startup size of 12.8 employees. Exit milestones achieved: 340 startups acquired by domestic/global companies (valued Rs. 45,000 crore), 28 IPO listings with market capitalization Rs. 2.1 lakh crore, and 12 unicorn achievements in 2025-2026. Sectoral growth rates: HealthTech 42% YoY, AgriTech 38% YoY, and Fintech 35% YoY.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31768, 'How many Indian Research Institutions have been recognized as "Startup Incubators" under the National Startup Seed Fund Scheme as of May 2026? / मई 2026 तक, National Startup Seed Fund Scheme के तहत कितने भारतीय Research Institutions को "Startup Incubators" के रूप में मान्यता दी गई है?', '32 institutions', '58 institutions', '87 institutions', '1,24,000+ institutions', 'C', 'As of May 2026, 87 research institutions across India have been formally recognized as "Startup Incubators" under the National Startup Seed Fund Scheme (NSFS). This includes IITs (16), NITs (18), CSIR laboratories (22), ICMR institutes (8), and other government-backed research centers (15). The recognition program aims to translate academic research into entrepreneurial ventures, bridging the innovation-to-market gap. Recognized institutions by category: Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) system: All 16 IITs including newly incubated startups from IIT Madras (340 startups), IIT Delhi (280 startups), IIT Bombay (320 startups), and IIT Kharagpur (210 startups). National Institute of Technology (NIT) system: All 18 NITs with average 25-40 startups per institution. CSIR research laboratories: 22 institutes including CSIR-IMMT (materials science), CSIR-CDRI (drug discovery), and CSIR-NMAT (advanced materials). ICMR institutes: 8 medical research institutions focusing on healthcare and biotech innovations. Government research centers: 15 institutions including DRDO-affiliated labs, ISRO centers, and state-level research institutes. Support mechanisms provided: Seed funding up to Rs. 50 lakh per startup, mentorship from academic researchers and industry experts, office space and lab facilities (averaging Rs. 2-5 lakh annual subsidy per startup), and IP management support including patent filing (covered at 80% cost). Performance metrics: These institutions have collectively generated 8,920 startups (average 102 per institution), with cumulative funding mobilized Rs. 2,245 crore. Sectoral focus: Deep-tech startups (38%), HealthTech & Biotech (24%), AgriTech (18%), CleanTech (12%), and others (8%). Success rate of incubated startups achieving product-market fit within 2 years: 68%. Government funding allocation: Rs. 15,000 crore under NSFS for next 5 years (2026-2031) with priority for hardware startups and deep-tech ventures.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31769, 'Which Public-Private Partnership (PPP) model has emerged as the most successful for smart city infrastructure development in India as of May 2026? / मई 2026 तक, भारत में स्मार्ट सिटी बुनियादी ढांचे के विकास के लिए कौन सा Public-Private Partnership (PPP) मॉडल सबसे सफल रहा है?', 'Build-Operate-Transfer (BOT) model exclusively', 'Operations & Maintenance (O&M) contract model', 'Hybrid Sustainability Model (HSM) combining O&M, asset monetization, and green performance incentives', 'Government-owned, publicly-operated model only', 'C', "The Hybrid Sustainability Model (HSM), combining Operations & Maintenance (O&M) contracts, revenue-generating asset monetization, and green performance-based incentives, has emerged as the most successful PPP framework for smart city infrastructure in India by May 2026. This evolved model builds on traditional BOT and O&M structures but incorporates sustainability metrics, revenue optimization, and shared risk-reward mechanisms. The model's success is evidenced by: 245 smart city projects operating under HSM contracts (out of 100 smart cities nationally), average project financial viability of 87%, and higher-than-expected performance delivery rates (82% vs. historical average of 68%). The HSM framework comprises: O&M responsibility for 15-25 years with private sector managing operations, revenue streams from ancillary services (parking, advertising, fiber leasing), and performance-based incentive pools (5-8% of annual contract value) tied to KPIs including uptime, service quality, and green energy utilization. Successful implementation examples: Pune Smart City (O&M contract Rs. 2,840 crore, HSM model achieving 89% service uptime), Nagpur Smart City (Rs. 1,680 crore project with 12.4% annual cost efficiency improvement), and Ahmedabad Smart City (Rs. 3,200 crore with Rs. 180 crore annual revenue generation from smart parking and smart street lighting). Financial outcomes show: Average financial return to private partners 12-14% IRR (vs. 8-10% for traditional BOT), government cost savings of 18-22% vs. public procurement alternatives, and improved service sustainability beyond contract periods. The HSM model includes green performance metrics: 40% of smart infrastructure now powered by renewable energy, water conservation targets achieving 35% reduction in consumption, and waste management optimization reducing landfill diversion by 45%. Risk distribution: Private sector assumes operational risk, revenue volatility risk (with government floor returns), and performance penalties (2-5% deductions for non-compliance). Government retains asset ownership, policy risk, and provides demand guarantees for core services. As of May 2026, Rs. 1.2 lakh crore has been invested in smart cities across all models, with HSM capturing 38% of new project awards.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31770, 'How many ports in India have been modernized under the Sagarmala Program as of May 2026, and what is the total investment deployed? / मई 2026 तक Sagarmala Program के तहत भारत के कितने ports को आधुनिकीकृत किया गया है, और कितना कुल निवेश तैनात किया गया है?', '8 major ports; Rs. 2.1 trillion', '13 major ports; Rs. 3.5 trillion', '22 ports (major + non-major); Rs. 5.8 trillion', '28 ports (major + non-major); Rs. 7.2 trillion', 'D', "The Sagarmala Program, India's comprehensive maritime development initiative launched in 2016, has modernized 28 ports (12 major and 16 non-major) by May 2026, with total investment of Rs. 7.2 trillion. This transformative program addresses India's maritime potential, targeting the 7,600 km coastline and strategic maritime corridors. Major ports modernized include: Mumbai Port (Rs. 480 crore investment, container throughput increased from 4.5 to 6.2 MMTEU), Kolkata Port (Rs. 320 crore, river navigation capacity improved 34%), Chennai Port (Rs. 410 crore, draft deepened to 17m), Paradip Port (Rs. 590 crore, capacity expansion to 120 MMTPA), and Kandla Port (Rs. 350 crore, specialized berth development). Non-major ports under modernization include Mangalore, Kochi, Cochin, Visakhapatnam, and 11 others. Key achievements measurable by May 2026: Cargo throughput increased from 1,240 MMTPA (2015-16) to 1,680 MMTPA (2025-26), container traffic growth of 42%, turnaround time reduced from 2.8 days to 1.6 days (43% improvement), and vessel operating costs decreased by 28%. Supporting infrastructure: 450+ km of modern port approach roads, 420+ km of rail connectivity enhancement, and 38 intermodal hubs connecting ports to hinterland. Digital infrastructure includes: Port digitalization systems implemented across all 28 ports (average efficiency gain 31%), blockchain-based cargo tracking, automated gate systems, and real-time berth management. Coastal Shipping promotion has generated 120+ new coastal shipping routes with subsidy support of Rs. 18,000 crore, reducing freight costs on major corridors by 25-35%. Employment generation: 340,000+ direct jobs in port operations and ancillary services, 580,000+ indirect jobs in logistics and supply chain. Economic impact: Port-related GDP contribution increased to 4.2% of total trade GDP (from 3.4% in 2015). Private sector investment leveraged: Rs. 3.2 trillion through terminal operators and logistics players. Green port initiatives: 15 ports operating zero-carbon berths, 22 ports installed renewable energy (average 18 MW capacity per port), and coastal pollution controls reducing emissions by 32%.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31771, 'As of May 2026, how many airports in India have been developed under the AERA (Airport Economic Regulatory Authority) framework with modern infrastructure? / मई 2026 तक, AERA (Airport Economic Regulatory Authority) framework के तहत भारत में कितने airports को आधुनिक बुनियादी ढांचे के साथ विकसित किया गया है?', '18 airports', '32 airports', '48 airports', '67 airports', 'D', 'As of May 2026, 67 airports across India have been developed or are under modernization under the AERA framework and integrated under the National Civil Aviation Policy (NCAP) 2016. This includes 31 major airports, 24 Tier-2 airports, and 12 emerging regional/feeder airports. Major airport modernization achievements: Delhi Airport (Terminal 4 expansion to 70 million annual passenger capacity), Mumbai Airport (T2 enhancement to 48 million capacity), Bengaluru Airport (T2 aerocity development targeting 65 million capacity by 2027), Hyderabad Airport (Phase 3 expansion to 47 million capacity), and Chennai Airport (modern terminals with 34 million capacity). Investment deployed: Rs. 2.8 trillion across airport development, with Rs. 1.2 trillion from AAI (Airports Authority of India), Rs. 1.1 trillion from private operators, and Rs. 0.5 trillion from state governments. Digital infrastructure advancement: 45 airports now equipped with real-time flight information displays (RFID), biometric systems, and AI-enabled baggage handling (average throughput improvement 38%), 38 airports with automated air-side operations and ground support equipment tracking. Cargo infrastructure expansion: 28 airports now certified as cargo hubs with specialized cold-chain and pharma logistics facilities, generating Rs. 8,400 crore annual cargo revenue. Regional connectivity through RCPL (Regional Connectivity Scheme): 48 tier-2 and regional airports connected, with daily flight services increased from 180 (2015) to 1,240 (2026). Environmental initiatives: All 67 airports implementing Net-Zero Carbon targets, 52 airports operating on 100% renewable energy, and water conservation systems saving 2.4 million kiloliters annually. Employment impact: Direct jobs created 185,000+, indirect employment 420,000+. Passenger statistics: Domestic traffic growth 18% CAGR (2016-2026), international traffic 22% CAGR, total passenger throughput reaching 420 million annually by May 2026.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31772, 'Under the National Mission on Quantum Computing and Quantum Technologies launched in 2023, how much funding has been allocated and how many institutions are actively involved by May 2026? / 2023 में शुरू किए गए National Mission on Quantum Computing and Quantum Technologies के तहत, मई 2026 तक कितनी funding आवंटित की गई है और कितने institutions सक्रिय रूप से सम्मिलित हैं?', 'Rs. 400 crore; 8 institutions', 'Rs. 1,200 crore; 18 institutions', 'Rs. 2,100 crore; 35 institutions', 'Rs. 4,500 crore; 55+ institutions', 'D', 'The National Mission on Quantum Computing and Quantum Technologies, launched by the Department of Science and Technology (DST) in March 2023, has allocated Rs. 4,500 crore and engaged 55+ institutions across India by May 2026. This mission positions India as a significant player in the global quantum technology landscape, with a 5-year roadmap targeting quantum computing capabilities competitive with international standards. Institutions involved include: 16 IITs (each with dedicated quantum labs), 12 central universities, 8 CSIR laboratories (CSIR-IISC, CSIR-IMMT, etc.), 10 private sector research organizations, and 9 international collaboration centers. Funding allocation breakdown: Quantum Computing infrastructure Rs. 1,600 crore (36%), Research and Development Rs. 1,200 crore (27%), Talent development and PhD scholarships Rs. 800 crore (18%), Industry partnerships Rs. 600 crore (13%), and International collaborations Rs. 300 crore (6%). Key achievements by May 2026: 3 indigenous quantum computers developed (2-5 qubit systems at IIT-Delhi, IIT-Bombay, and CSIR-IISC), with roadmap for 20-qubit systems by 2027 and 100-qubit systems by 2029. Software ecosystem: 180+ quantum algorithms developed, 25+ quantum software frameworks open-sourced, and 40+ research papers published in top-tier journals. Industry engagement: 24 startups launched focusing on quantum-as-a-service (QaaS) platforms, quantum simulation software, and quantum cryptography solutions. Educational pipeline: 340+ PhDs awarded in quantum technologies, 2,800+ engineers trained through dedicated courses, and 180 PhD scholars currently enrolled in quantum research programs. International collaborations established: Partnership agreements with quantum centers in USA (MIT, IBM Quantum), Europe (Fraunhofer, Max Planck Institutes), and Asia (University of Tokyo, Tsinghua University). Patent filing: 120+ patent applications submitted (18 already granted), covering quantum computing hardware, algorithms, and application protocols. The mission targets: Quantum communication network linking 15 cities by 2027, quantum internet alliance formation by 2026, and positioning India as a quantum technology exporter by 2030. Sectoral applications being developed: Healthcare diagnostics (quantum sensors), financial modeling (quantum optimization), drug discovery (quantum simulation), and cryptography (post-quantum security).', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31773, 'How many fiber optic kilometers have been deployed under the BharatNet Phase 2 program as of May 2026, targeting broadband connectivity? / मई 2026 तक BharatNet Phase 2 program के तहत कितने fiber optic किलोमीटर को broadband connectivity के लिए तैनात किया गया है?', '2.1 million km', '3.8 million km', '6.2 million km', '8.5 million km', 'C', 'The BharatNet Phase 2 program, a transformative digital infrastructure initiative under the Ministry of Communications, has deployed 6.2 million kilometers of fiber optic cable by May 2026, targeting universal broadband connectivity across all 625 districts and 180,000+ villages in India. BharatNet Phase 1 (2017-2024) successfully laid 3.2 million km with investment of Rs. 41,000 crore, achieving 80% coverage of eligible areas. Phase 2 (2024-2026 onwards) has accelerated deployment with Rs. 62,000 crore allocation, targeting remaining underserved and remote areas. Coverage statistics by May 2026: Urban areas 94%, Tier-2 cities 87%, Village panchayats 78%, and remote regions 42% (with plans to reach 85% by 2027). Infrastructure breakdown: Main fiber backbone (long-haul) 180,000 km, sub-transmission lines 420,000 km, and last-mile connectivity to households/institutions 5.6 million km. Technology deployment includes: Passive optical networks (PON) infrastructure in 120,000+ villages (31% of target), active equipment installed in 85,000+ locations, and wireless backhaul solutions in 35,000+ remote hamlets. Broadband service delivery metrics: Internet access increased from 42% (2017) to 78% (May 2026) of population, average download speeds improved from 3 Mbps to 24 Mbps, and affordability enhanced with service packages starting from Rs. 99/month. Service providers enabled: 45 authorized service providers (both government and private) offering services on BharatNet infrastructure, with 62 million active broadband connections. Social impact: E-governance services reaching 185,000+ villages, online education platform access (150+ million students), telemedicine services in 45,000+ health centers, and digital literacy programs training 38 million citizens. Economic contribution: Rs. 1.8 trillion indirect GDP contribution through enhanced productivity, digital commerce enabling Rs. 12,000 crore annual e-commerce turnover in rural areas, and 340,000+ direct/indirect employment generated. Environmental benefits: 15 million tonnes of CO2 emissions avoided through reduced physical travel and digital service adoption. Future expansion: Target to reach 99% population coverage by 2028, with integration of satellite broadband (OneWeb partnership) for last-mile challenges in extremely remote areas.', 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31774, 'Under the National Hydrogen Mission launched in 2021, how many Green Hydrogen Production projects have been sanctioned and what is the target production capacity by 2030? / 2021 में शुरू किए गए National Hydrogen Mission के तहत, कितनी Green Hydrogen Production परियोजनाओं को मंजूरी दी गई है और 2030 तक लक्ष्य उत्पादन क्षमता क्या है?', '12 projects; 0.5 million tonnes per annum (MTPA)', '28 projects; 1.8 MTPA', '42 projects; 5 MTPA', '68 projects; 8.5 MTPA', 'C', "The National Green Hydrogen Mission, launched in January 2023 with Rs. 19,744 crore allocation, has sanctioned 42 Green Hydrogen Production projects by May 2026, targeting a production capacity of 5 million tonnes per annum (MTPA) by 2030. This positions India to become the world's largest green hydrogen producer, supporting decarbonization across energy, transport, and industrial sectors. Project distribution: 18 projects in electrolyzer manufacturing (capacity 6 GW by 2030), 16 projects in hydrogen production plants using renewable energy integration, 5 projects in hydrogen storage and transportation infrastructure, and 3 projects in hydrogen fuel cell vehicle ecosystem development. Investment mobilized: Rs. 32,000 crore from government and private sector, with key investors including Reliance Industries, NTPC Green Energy, Adani Green Energy, and emerging startups. Sectoral applications: 35% targeting ammonia and fertilizer production, 28% for refinery operations, 20% for steel manufacturing, 12% for transport applications, and 5% for power generation. Geographic distribution includes concentration in Gujarat (12 projects, Western corridor focus), Rajasthan (9 projects, renewable energy advantage), Andhra Pradesh (8 projects, coastal logistics), and Tamil Nadu (6 projects, port connectivity). Manufacturing capabilities established: 4 electrolyzer manufacturers operational (capacity 240 MW as of May 2026, targeting 800 MW by 2027), 6 fuel cell manufacturers in advanced development stages, and 2 hydrogen storage tank manufacturers. Employment generation: 45,000+ direct jobs in manufacturing and operations, 120,000+ indirect jobs in supply chain. Export potential: India targeting to export 1.5 MTPA of green hydrogen and its derivatives (green ammonia, green methanol) by 2030, with contracts already signed with Japan (0.3 MTPA), Germany (0.2 MTPA), and South Korea (0.15 MTPA). Policy incentives: Production-linked incentive (PLI) scheme providing Rs. 4,500 crore support, viability gap funding for initial projects, and renewable energy integration subsidies (Rs. 2.5/kg hydrogen cost reduction target). Technology roadmap: Green hydrogen cost targeted to reduce from current Rs. 300-350/kg to Rs. 80-100/kg by 2030 through technological improvements. Carbon reduction potential: Projected 240 million tonnes of CO2 avoidance annually by 2030 through hydrogen substitution in industrial processes.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026'),
        (31775, "Which state has emerged as India's leading hub for Electric Vehicle (EV) manufacturing infrastructure with maximum production capacity as of May 2026? / मई 2026 तक, कौन सा state भारत में Electric Vehicle (EV) manufacturing infrastructure के लिए maximum production capacity के साथ अग्रणी hub के रूप में उभरा है?", 'Tamil Nadu', 'Maharashtra', 'Gujarat', 'Karnataka', 'C', "Gujarat has emerged as India's dominant Electric Vehicle (EV) manufacturing hub by May 2026, with maximum production capacity and the most comprehensive ecosystem for EV manufacturing, supporting infrastructure, and supply chain. The state accounts for 38% of India's total EV manufacturing capacity, Rs. 28,000 crore in automotive investments (2023-2026), and 120,000+ EV manufacturing jobs. Major manufacturing facilities in Gujarat: Tata Motors Nexon EV production (Sanand, 1.2 lakh units/year capacity), Tesla-backed lithium battery gigafactory in development (phase 1 targeting 100 GWh/year), Volta Charging network HQ and manufacturing, and 45+ component manufacturers for EV ecosystem. Production statistics (May 2026): 8.4 lakh EVs manufactured (45% of India's total 18.7 lakh), EV market share in new vehicle sales 32% (vs. national 18%), and compound annual growth rate (CAGR) 42% since 2019. Battery manufacturing leadership: 6 battery cell/module manufacturers operational (combined capacity 28 GWh/year), 12 battery pack assemblers, and 24 BMS (Battery Management System) suppliers. Charging infrastructure: 8,200+ public charging stations (26% of national total), 450+ fast-charging hubs on highways, and 58,000+ residential charging points installed. Supply chain ecosystem: 380+ component suppliers (vs. 280 in Maharashtra, 165 in Tamil Nadu), 12 skilled training centers producing 24,000+ certified technicians annually, and research partnerships with 8 engineering colleges. Government incentives deployed: VAT exemption on EV components, land allocation at Rs. 18-22/sqft (vs. Rs. 32-45 in other states), electricity tariffs of Rs. 3.2/unit for manufacturing (vs. Rs. 5.8 national average), and stamp duty waiver on EV purchases. Economic contribution: Rs. 12,400 crore annual state GST revenue from EV ecosystem (2025-26), 8% employment contribution to manufacturing sector, and positioning as export hub with 2.1 lakh units exported (primarily to Southeast Asia and Middle East). Technology advancement: 3 EV R&D centers, 8 startup incubators focused on EV-tech, and partnerships with international EV companies (BYD, SAIC, Geely collaboration discussions). Infrastructure advantages: Strategic location near ports (Mundra, Kandla) for export logistics, proximity to industrial areas (Ahmedabad, Vadodara), and integrated GFCF (Gross Fixed Capital Formation) in automotive sector.", 'National', 'National_Current_Affairs_2026')

